## A. DECISIONS TO CONFIRM (read this first)

**Three decisions are already closed** and are now project policy, not questions. **The three are D-02, D-07 and D-11 — the same three rows the C-table below marks CLOSED**, and D-11 is closed **by construction** (the answer is already executed in four places) rather than by a fresh student decision:

| Closed | Answer | Where it is now fixed |
|---|---|---|
| **D-02 — Compute for the local model** | **School / laboratory machines are available.** The "one student laptop, nothing else" constraint is **relaxed for compute.** | Sections E.1, J (D-02) |
| **D-07 — AI permission depth** | **Read-only by default; changes only with human approval.** Mutating tools exist but are gated behind an explicit approval workflow with a full audit log. | Sections E.4, E.5, E.8, I.8 (governing policy) |
| **D-11 — Is Packet Tracer kept at all?** | **(b) — kept, strictly as a design-time tool**, labelled as such in every figure; nothing automates it. **Closed by construction:** the answer is already executed in four places (§C.3's Figure 1, §C.4, §J-D-11 and §M item 2). | Section C.3, C.4, J (D-11) |

**D-13 (offline-only or hybrid) is deliberately NOT in that list.** Its recommendation is firm — **(a) strictly local/offline as the delivered configuration, with (b) permitted only as a documented contingency** (§E.1.5) — but the row itself is still **open with an "end of week 2" deadline** in the C-table below, §J-D-13 is written as a decision still to be taken, and §M item 6 still asks the group to answer it. **A recommendation is not a closed decision, and this list names only the three rows the table actually marks CLOSED.**

**Four supervisor-level questions have now been answered by the student, and this outline is edited to record the answers rather than the questions.** They are transcribed in §A.1 and they change the body in four places:

- **SQ-1 — answered, and it ratifies a direction.** The Cisco layer **must** become genuinely reachable and AI-manageable (primary platform **CML**; Packet Tracer **cannot** be the AI-managed layer), but **management is introduced incrementally rather than all at once** — 「期望係完全管理，可逐步加入，非一步到位」. The new **§C.6 staged AI-coverage ramp** is the mechanism, and it turns the ordering already implied by §D.3 and §F into explicit entry and exit gates.
- **SQ-2 — answered, and the answer is a deferral, not a refusal.** School/lab hardware is expected to exist, but **the specific machine and GPU will be arranged when the student returns to Hong Kong** — 「未定，但應有硬件資源，需要待我返港才可安排」. The GPU-dependent decisions (**B1**, **B3**, **D-04**) therefore move from *blocking now* to *blocking at a scheduled point*: gate **G2-H at week 6**, where the hardware record is captured, the tier is selected, and the model class is finally pinned down. Nothing before that gate needs the specification (§E.1.1a) — **B2 is the only thing that blocks work now.**
- **SQ-4 — answered: yes.** The **AI / MCP layer is the assessed differentiator**, and the old *"the AI line is the weakest line"* framing is **withdrawn as a ratified position** (§L.4 W4). The network remains the foundation the differentiator depends on.
- **SQ-5 — answered: the proposal alone, for now** — 「暫時只做 proposal」. The immediate submission is the **proposal**; the **90,000–110,000-word document is a later, fuller submission** with its own size expectations (§H.3).

**Only ONE item still blocks work starting now — B2, and nothing else.** Everything that was formerly listed as "blocking" is now a **scheduled** gate, and §M item 1 states the same count:

| Blocking now | Why it blocks | By when |
|---|---|---|
| **B2 — Ratification of the Cisco platform recommendation** (Section C.4 / J-D-01: **primary CML, fallback hybrid**) | `MCP-S-CISCO` — one whole domain of the AI layer — cannot be designed until the Cisco layer is known to be reachable. **SQ-1 confirms the direction (the layer must become genuinely manageable), so what remains is the platform record itself** | end of week 1 |

**Three further items are blocked, but at a scheduled gate rather than now** — the student's hardware decision is deferred until they return to Hong Kong, and nothing before week 6 needs it. **B3 is one of them**, because the model class is derived from B1 and D-04 and cannot be settled before they are:

| Blocked at a gate | Why it blocks | Gate and week |
|---|---|---|
| **B3 — The model class** (derived from B1, then measured) | Decides which use cases are achievable at all. **It is derived from B1 and D-04, so it cannot be settled before the G2-H hardware record exists — and it is a measurement rather than a choice made in advance** | **G2-H, end of week 6** (the tier is selected from the record and the week-5–6 spike's malformed-call rate names the model); **confirmed at G3 (week 8)**, where the malformed-call rate is measured against the chosen model |
| **B1 — The lab machine's exact specification** (GPU model, VRAM, system RAM, disk, OS, whether it is shared/bookable, whether outbound network access is permitted, and the host of the Cisco platform) | **The choice of model is blocked on this.** No specification means no defensible model class, no honest sizing table and no credible tool-calling claim. **The specification capture must therefore identify BOTH hosts**: the **model host** (where the local model is served) and the **CML / Cisco-platform host** (where Cisco Modeling Labs runs its virtual IOS-XE nodes). They may be the same machine or two machines, but §C.4's own cost figure — CML costs *"~ 16–32 GB RAM for a small topology on top of the hypervisor"* — means the Cisco platform host must be named, sized and recorded, and the D-03 no-co-location rule must be checked against it. **The deferral changes the date, not the requirement: every field above is still captured and written down — at G2-H, before the AI line starts in week 7, and never replaced by a model chosen on reputation** | **G2-H, end of week 6** (was end of week 1). §E.1.1a states what proceeds meanwhile |
| **D-04 — The GPU / VRAM tier available** (the substance of B1) | The tier determines the model class and therefore which use cases are achievable. **The tier table (T0–T4) and the no-GPU fallback at §E.1.2/§E.1.3 are unchanged and still correct; only the timing moves.** The row is no longer an immediate blocker | **G2-H, end of week 6** (was end of week 1). See §A's D-04 row and §J-D-04 |

