## C. Target architecture

### C.1 The three device families

| Family | Platform | Count (target) | Management interfaces that the AI plane may use |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Windows** | Windows Server guests (members of one AD DS domain) | **9** (`vpnz-dc01`, `vpnz-dc02`, `vpnz-fs01`, `vpnz-san01`, `vpnz-rras01`, `vpnz-dmzweba01`, `vpnz-dmzweba02`, `vpnz-jump01`, `vpnz-brdc01`) | WinRM (5985/5986), PowerShell Remoting, RPC-based AD/FSRM cmdlets, SNMPv3 agent, Windows Event Log, `windows_exporter` (9182), syslog via agent |
| **Linux** | Ubuntu Server guests | **10** (`vpnz-mon01`, `vpnz-ai01`, `vpnz-lnxra01`, `vpnz-nft01`, `vpnz-nft02`, `vpnz-dmzrev01`, `vpnz-dmzrev02`, `vpnz-dmzwp01`, `vpnz-dmzwp02`, `vpnz-dmzjump01`) | SSH (22), `systemctl`/`journalctl`, nftables, `node_exporter` (9100), rsyslog forward (514/udp) |
| **Cisco** | **Virtual IOS-XE/IOS devices with real management planes** (see Section C.4) | **5 target, 8 at most** — the target is fixed by **D-16**: one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall | **SSH (22) for `show` commands** and **SNMPv3 (161/162) are the two guaranteed interfaces** — §C.6's Stage B and §B.3 C1 are both written on exactly these two. **RESTCONF (443) is conditional on the image** and is required only "where the image supports it"; classic IOS lacks it (§C.4 option (e)). Syslog (514), NetFlow if licensed and NTP for clock integrity are supporting interfaces, not the management-plane guarantee |
| **AI plane** | One Ubuntu host (model serving + MCP servers) and one agent host, or one host with separated service identities | 2 | — (it is the consumer, not the managed element) |

**The estate count, stated once so that nothing below has to re-derive it.** The frozen baseline's §6/§15.1 inventory is **36 rows = 16 Packet Tracer logical devices + 19 virtual machines + 1 hypervisor host**; the 19 VMs are **9 Windows Server guests + 10 Ubuntu guests** (`vpnz-hvhost01` is the physical host, not a guest). **That 19-VM / 10-Linux / 9-Windows estate is the number this outline carries forward and the number §C.2b, §D.2, §D.3, §F.2, §L.1 and §L.1a all use.** Where a section needs to name a *subset* — §D.2's MUST core, or §C.2b's matrix — it says so explicitly and the subset is still within the 19. **No section of this outline may state a different estate size.**

### C.2 How they interconnect

- The **VM estate's segmentation** is delivered by hypervisor host-only networks, one per zone, DHCP disabled on every one of them (carried over from the frozen baseline: VLAN 20 management `10.20.20.0/24`, VLAN 30 server `10.10.30.0/24`, VLAN 50 egress transit `10.10.50.0/28`, VLAN 90 DMZ `10.10.90.0/24`, VLAN 80 storage `10.10.80.0/24`). This is an **emulated** segmentation: the outcome is real, the mechanism is not 802.1Q.
- The **Cisco layer** provides the routed core, the WAN links, the branch sites, and the perimeter. It connects to the VM estate through one or more layer-3 handoff segments (the baseline's `TRANSIT-EGRESS` and `LAN-HQ-CORE` concepts are reused for this purpose).
- **Cross-family traffic** flows routed, never bridged. `172.16.0.0/16` and `192.168.0.0/16` remain unallocated, and `.local`/`.lan`/`contoso`/`example.com` remain banned strings.
- **The AI plane sits in the management zone (VLAN 20 / `Z-MGMT`)**, not in the server zone, and every one of its outbound connections is an allow-listed management protocol to a named host. It has no route to the isolated lab segment.

### C.2a THE REACHABILITY RULE — stated once, unambiguously

> ## No MCP tool, AI agent, Ansible playbook or script in this project may claim to reach, read from, or configure a Cisco Packet Tracer device.
>
> **Packet Tracer devices are NOT REACHABLE.** Packet Tracer exposes **no SSH server reachable by external tooling, no SNMP agent a real poller can query, no NETCONF endpoint and no RESTCONF endpoint, and no management API whatsoever.** Any sentence, table row, diagram arrow, tool description or test case that implies otherwise is a fabrication and must be deleted.

**Why this is stated here, at the top, rather than buried in a limitations chapter.** The existing proposal corpus contains a **real internal contradiction** on exactly this point, and it must be resolved by this outline rather than inherited:

| Where | What it says | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| `sections\06-automation.md` §10.7 | *"Packet Tracer has no API, no SSH server reachable from real tooling, and no Ansible collection. No playbook, script or tool in this project can log in to a Packet Tracer device. Any statement to the contrary would be fabrication."* | **CORRECT — keep this, and lift the same discipline into the AI chapter** |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.6.3 | Defines a tool `get_interface_stats` as an *"SNMPv3 authPriv poll of the Packet Tracer device estate"* | **WRONG — withdraw.** The AI chapter contradicts the automation chapter |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.6.4; baseline §12.5 | A data-flow diagram feeding the agent from the Packet Tracer devices | **WRONG — withdraw.** Redraw so that no arrow points at a Packet Tracer device |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.7.2 | A literal `snmpwalk … <validated-device-ip>` against the device estate | **WRONG — withdraw**, unless the target is a genuinely reachable device |
| `sections\05-cisco.md` §9.2.4 | Packet Tracer SNMPv3 is *"unreliable"* and may be evidenced *"only on the server estate"* | **CORRECT and decisive** — the Cisco chapter's own support table already concedes the point that the AI chapter ignored |

**The rule that follows, and that governs every later writer:** *an interface exists for this project only if a command run from the management host, in this lab, returned data from that specific device, and the transcript is filed as evidence.* Anything else is a design intention and must be labelled as one.

### C.2b Device-reachability matrix (normative)

Every managed device in the estate appears here with **the concrete interface that makes it reachable**. This matrix replaces the old design's service-only port matrix as the authority for what the AI layer can touch. A device that is not in this matrix is not a managed device, and **no tool may be written against it**.

`REACHABLE` = **a transcript is filed showing a command run from the management host, in this lab, returning data from that specific device through a real interface.** Existence is not enough and a design intention is not enough: the evidence-based rule at §C.2a governs, and this legend states the same rule in the same tense. `CONDITIONALLY REACHABLE` = the interface is designed and is expected to exist, but it depends on an **open decision (named in the row)** and therefore **no transcript can exist yet**; the row becomes `REACHABLE` only when the decision is ratified and the transcript is filed, and until then **no tool may be written against it**. `NOT REACHABLE` = no interface exists in this project; nothing may claim to reach it.

| # | Device / family | Platform | **Status** | Reachability interface (the concrete mechanism) | What the AI layer may therefore do |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| RW-01 | `vpnz-dc01`, `vpnz-dc02` | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **WinRM over HTTPS 5986** (PowerShell Remoting, `svc_aiagent`); `windows_exporter` 9182 scrape; Event Log via WinRM; syslog agent 514/udp | Read service state, event IDs, AD objects, replication health. **Read only — never a write (N4)** |
| RW-02 | `vpnz-fs01` | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-01, plus **RPC-based FSRM cmdlets** (`Get-FsrmQuota`, `Get-FsrmFileScreen`) over WinRM | Read quota and file-screen configuration; read disk usage |
| RW-03 | **Every Windows member server not named in RW-01/RW-02** — complete enumeration: `vpnz-san01`, `vpnz-rras01`, `vpnz-dmzweba01`, `vpnz-dmzweba02`, `vpnz-jump01`, `vpnz-brdc01` (all 6 named; **9 of 9 Windows guests are covered** once RW-01's two DCs and RW-02's `vpnz-fs01` are added) | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-01 | Read service, event and disk state |
| RW-04 | `vpnz-mon01` (metrics, logs, Git, Ansible) | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **HTTPS API** (Prometheus 9090, Loki, Grafana 3000 read), **Git over SSH**, local filesystem read for the config archive | Query metrics, logs, alerts, inventory, drift baselines, change history |
| RW-05 | `vpnz-nft01`, `vpnz-nft02` | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **SSH 22** with a forced-command read-only allow-list (`nft list ruleset`, `ip -s link`, `systemctl show`, `journalctl`); `node_exporter` 9100; rsyslog 514/udp | Read the ruleset, interface counters, service state and the **drop log** — the richest AI signal in the estate |
| RW-06 | **Every Ubuntu guest not named in RW-04/RW-05** — complete enumeration: `vpnz-dmzrev01`, `vpnz-dmzrev02`, `vpnz-lnxra01`, `vpnz-dmzwp01`, `vpnz-dmzwp02`, `vpnz-dmzjump01` (all 6 named; **10 of 10 Ubuntu guests are covered** once RW-04's `vpnz-mon01`, RW-05's `vpnz-nft01/02` and RW-09's `vpnz-ai01` are added) | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-05 | Read service state, logs, disk, config files (allow-listed, secrets redacted) |
| RW-07 | Cisco devices — **CML or physical, per D-01** (never Packet Tracer) | Genuine IOS-XE / IOS | **CONDITIONALLY REACHABLE** *(pending D-01 and D-12)* | **SSH 22** (`show`-only allow-list, no config mode); **SNMPv3 authPriv 161/162**; **RESTCONF over HTTPS 443** where the image supports it; syslog 514/udp; NetFlow if licensed | Read routing, switching, ACL, interface, crypto and log state; read YANG-modelled configuration. **Never enter configuration mode (N5)** |
| RW-08 | `VP-HQ-FW-01` (ASA-class perimeter) | Genuine ASA / ASAv | **CONDITIONALLY REACHABLE** *(pending D-01)* | **SSH**, **SNMPv3**, syslog; `show running-config`, `show access-list`, `show crypto ipsec sa` | Read ACL/NAT/crypto state and the deny log |
| RW-09 | `vpnz-ai01` / the agent host | Ubuntu VM | **REACHABLE** (self) | Local process control only | Its own audit log and health. **It is not a managed network element** |
| **NR-01** | **All Packet Tracer devices** — `VP-HQ-CORE-01/02`, `VP-HQ-DSW-A01..A04`, `VP-HQ-EDGE-01`, `VP-HQ-FW-01`, `VP-BR-*`, `VP-MO-*`, `VP-WC-*`, `VP-ISP-EDGE-01`, `VP-HQ-VOICE-01` | **Cisco Packet Tracer (simulator)** | **NOT REACHABLE** | **None. No SSH, no SNMP, no NETCONF, no RESTCONF, no API.** | **Nothing.** No MCP tool, no agent, no playbook, no script, no test case. Packet Tracer may appear only as a **design-time** artefact, drawn with a crossed-out arrow and labelled *"no reachable management interface"* |
| **NR-01a** | **`VP-ISP-EDGE-01` — the eBGP AS-65000 simulated provider** (the peer required by §B.3 C2 and §D.1.4) | **Cisco Packet Tracer, as recorded in the baseline's §13.2 PT-09** (*"PT cannot simulate a real ISP"*) | **NOT REACHABLE** | **None.** The provider is a simulation object: it has no SSH, SNMP, NETCONF, RESTCONF or API surface, and it is **not** the same device as `VP-HQ-EDGE-01` | **Nothing — and this has a specific cost that must be stated:** **the AI layer can never verify the eBGP session from the peer side.** `show ip bgp summary` and `show ip route bgp` on the *local* edge router remain readable and remain the verification the project will use, but **no MCP tool, agent, playbook or test may claim to have observed the provider's own view of the session, its configuration, or its route table.** If the group wants a peer-side view it must make the provider a **genuinely reachable** device (a second CML IOS-XE node used as AS-65000, per D-01), in which case that node joins **RW-07** and is evidenced like any other `REACHABLE` row |
| NR-02 | The hypervisor host's management plane | VMware/VirtualBox | **NOT REACHABLE** from the AI layer | Deliberately excluded | Nothing. Kept out of the blast radius by design |
| NR-03 | The isolated lab segment (`10.70.0.0/16`) | — | **NOT REACHABLE** | No route | Nothing. It is the designated source for deliberate-misuse tests only |

**Verification obligation that goes with this matrix.** For **every** `REACHABLE` row, a transcript is filed showing the interface returning data from that device (for example a `Test-WSMan` result, an `snmpwalk -v3` result, a `show version` result, a Prometheus target marked `up`). **A `REACHABLE` row with no transcript is a claim, not a fact, and the report must list it as unverified.** This is the same evidence discipline the automation chapter already applies to Ansible, applied to the AI layer.

**The matrix is a deliverable.** It becomes a table in the design chapter, a test family in the verification chapter (`TC-REACH-…`), and — critically — it is what makes `MCP-S-CISCO` either a real server or an honest omission. If RW-07 and RW-08 are not reachable, then `MCP-S-CISCO` **cannot exist**, and the scope reduction in Section C.5 applies.

**Estate coverage — the completeness statement this matrix previously left implicit.** §L.1 promises that the VLAN/zone plan is carried over for continuity, and **§L.1a item 3 promises that the baseline's device and VM inventory — 36 rows, of which 19 are virtual machines — is carried forward essentially as written.** §C.2b therefore cannot quietly cover fewer hosts than that inventory contains. The coverage is:

| Inventory class | Count in the frozen baseline (§6/§15.1) | Covered in this matrix | Where |
|---|---|---|---|
| Windows Server guests | **9** | **9 of 9** | RW-01 (2), RW-02 (1), RW-03 (6) |
| Ubuntu guests | **10** | **10 of 10** | RW-04 (1), RW-05 (2), RW-06 (6), RW-09 (1) |
| Hypervisor host | **1** | 1, deliberately excluded from AI management | **NR-02** |
| Packet Tracer logical devices | **16** | 16, deliberately excluded from AI management | **NR-01** |
| eBGP simulated provider | (one of the 16 PT devices: `VP-ISP-EDGE-01`) | 1, deliberately excluded — see NR-01a | **NR-01a** |
| Cisco devices the AI plane manages | 5 target (D-16) | 5–8, **conditional** | RW-07, RW-08 |
| **Total inventory rows** | **36** | **36 accounted for: every row is either a `REACHABLE`/`CONDITIONALLY REACHABLE` row or a named `NOT REACHABLE` row** | — |

**Every inventory member is therefore either in the matrix or named as excluded in it, and none is silently absent.** The two exclusions that are decisions rather than omissions are `NR-02` (the hypervisor host's management plane — kept out of the blast radius, and consistent with the CML question below) and `NR-01`/`NR-01a` (the Packet Tracer layer, including the simulated eBGP provider — excluded because it exposes no interface at all).

**How this matrix is consumed by the staged AI-coverage ramp (§C.6).** The matrix is the **register**; §C.6 is the **order in which its rows are brought under AI management**, with an entry and an exit gate at each stage. **The rows move as a group, and only in this order:** §C.6 **Stage A** covers **RW-01 … RW-06 and RW-09** (the server estate); **Stage B** covers **one** device of the kind **RW-07** will hold; **Stage C** covers the **five D-16 devices** across **RW-07/RW-08**; and **Stage D** is the optional conversion of **NR-01a** into an **RW-07** row so the eBGP session can be verified from the peer side. **Two consequences follow, and neither is optional:** a row may not be advanced to a later stage by design intent — **only by a filed transcript, per this matrix's own verification obligation above** — and **nothing at all may be claimed from `NR-01` at any stage**, because that row has no interface for any stage to reach.

**The one consequence of the eBGP exclusion that the report must state out loud (§B.3 C2 and §D.1.4 both require exterior routing).** The estate routes to an **AS-65000 simulated provider** that the baseline records as a Packet Tracer object (`PT-09`: *"PT cannot simulate a real ISP"*). That makes the provider **NR-01a**, and it means **the AI layer can never verify the BGP session from the peer side**: the agent can read `show ip bgp summary`, `show ip route bgp` and the syslog/NetFlow of the *local* edge router, but it can never see the provider's own state, configuration or view of the session, because the provider has no interface to read. **No tool, test case or figure may imply otherwise.** The honest framing for the report is: *"the eBGP session was verified from the local autonomous system only; the simulated provider exposes no management interface, so no peer-side verification is claimed."* If a peer-side view is required, the provider must become a genuinely reachable CML IOS-XE node under **D-01**, and then it is a `REACHABLE` row under RW-07 with its own transcript.

