### C.4 The Cisco layer must be reachable — the decisive treatment

**The problem, stated plainly.** Cisco Packet Tracer is a teaching simulator. Its devices present a CLI inside the application and **no reachable SSH server, no SNMP agent that a real poller can query, and no NETCONF or RESTCONF endpoint**. Therefore:

> **No MCP server, Ansible playbook, SNMP poller or AI agent can reach a Packet Tracer device. Any design that places the AI-managed Cisco layer inside Packet Tracer cannot demonstrate AI network management. This is not a tuning problem; it is a category error.**

**One device this decision does not place, and the consequence of that.** §B.3 C2 requires exterior routing and §D.1.4 makes **eBGP to a simulated provider** a SHOULD. That provider — the AS-65000 peer — is a **Cisco device the estate routes to**, and in the frozen baseline it is a Packet Tracer object (§13.2 PT-09: **PT cannot simulate a real ISP**). It is therefore **`NR-01a`** in the matrix: **not reachable, and never claimable as reachable.** The specific cost, which must be written down rather than discovered at marking time: **the AI layer can never verify the BGP session from the peer side.** The agent may read `show ip bgp summary` and `show ip route bgp` from the **local** edge router and correlate those with syslog and NetFlow, but it can never observe the provider's own view, configuration or route table, because the provider exposes no interface at all. **If a peer-side view is wanted, the provider must be made a genuinely reachable device** — a second CML IOS-XE node configured as AS-65000, sanctioned by D-01 and evidenced as an RW-07 row — and **that decision must be taken in week 1**, because it changes the device count and the CML licence requirement (D-12/D-16). **The week-1 requirement is on the decision — the licence and entitlement question — which is D-12's and needs no hardware, and it is not on the build; that is the distinction that resolves the apparent conflict.** The node itself is **built only after Stage C is evidenced** (§C.6): §C.6's Stage D entry gate is *"Stage C is exited"*, Stage C closes at G4 in **week 10**, and Stage D therefore falls in **weeks 10–12** and is explicitly **not required within the 12 weeks**. **A week-1 decision and a week-10 build are not in tension: the decision fixes the count and the licence ask, and the build is what waits.** This is stated in full at §C.2b.

**The Cisco device count this decision must fix — and the count the rest of the document uses.** The three figures that previously disagreed were **§C.1's 5–8**, **§D.2's 5–6**, and **§C.4's own primary topology, which implies 5**. **D-16 settles it: the CML target is five devices** — one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall — **rising to at most eight only if every one of them can be evidenced as `REACHABLE` in §C.2b**; under the hybrid fallback the reachable count is **2–4**. Because §C.2b is normative, the count follows the evidence and not the ambition. **Every other section of this outline uses the D-16 figures.**

**The six candidates, assessed on the criteria that actually decide this.**

Each candidate is scored **✔ strong / ~ workable with a stated cost / ✘ disqualifying for this project**.

| Criterion | (a) CML | (b) GNS3 + genuine images | (c) EVE-NG | (d) DevNet Sandbox | (e) Physical 1841/2960-class | (f) Hybrid: PT + 2–4 reachable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Real management interfaces** (SSH / SNMPv3 / RESTCONF) | ✔ SSH, SNMP, RESTCONF on supporting IOS-XE images | ✔ same, if the image supports it | ✔ same, if the image supports it | ✔ real devices, real APIs | ✔ SSH + SNMP on classic IOS; **RESTCONF ✘** (too old) | ✔ (from whichever reachable option is chosen) |
| **Licensing and cost** | ~ paid personal/education licence; entitlement to confirm (D-12) | ~ **software free; the IOS images are the licensing problem** | ~ same image problem | ✔ free with an account | ~ real money, small; plus space, noise, power, console cables | ~ cost of the small reachable set only |
| **Persists across sessions** (can you archive the built network?) | ✔ the topology and images are yours | ✔ yours | ✔ yours | ✘ **reserved instances are time-boxed and reset** | ✔ it is a physical object on a desk | ✔ |
| **Fidelity to real IOS / IOS-XE** | ✔ genuine Cisco images on the official platform | ~ genuine images, but the virtualisation layer is third-party and CAN be made to behave oddly | ~ as GNS3 | ✔ genuine | ~ genuine but **old** feature sets and old crypto | ~ split: high in PT for topology, genuine on the reachable few |
| **Support for the named protocols** OSPF / EIGRP / BGP / RSTP | ✔ all four, natively | ✔ all four (RSTP depends on the switch image — IOS routers do not do switching) | ✔ as GNS3 | ✔ all four | ✔ OSPF/EIGRP/BGP and RSTP on a 2960; **ASA ✘** | ✔ |
| **Can host an ASA / firewall equivalent** | ✔ ASAv-class node available on the platform | ~ possible with the right image, but the ASAv image is itself licence-encumbered | ~ as GNS3 | ✔ where the sandbox topology includes one | ✘ a 1841/2960 set has no ASA; a used ASA 5505/5506 is a separate purchase | ~ depends on the chosen reachable option; otherwise the perimeter is emulated |
| **Local resource cost** | ~ 16–32 GB RAM for a small topology on top of the hypervisor | ~ similar or heavier; the VM images are large | ~ heaviest of the three (full VM per node) | ✔ **zero local compute** | ✔ zero | ✔ lowest of the virtual options (PT is nearly free, a few devices are cheap) |
| **Examinable / evidence-friendly** (screenshot, transcript, re-run) | ✔ `show version`, `show run`, SNMP walks, RESTCONF JSON, all capturable | ✔ same | ✔ same | ~ capturable, **but you cannot promise the same device tomorrow** | ✔ the strongest evidence there is: a real device you own, photographed and archived | ✔ strong, provided every figure is labelled simulated-vs-real |
| **Likely to be permitted by the school** | ✔ official Cisco platform, licensed | ~ **the image-provenance question is the risk** | ~ same risk | ~ hosted third-party resource forming part of a graded submission — **ask, do not assume** | ✔ it is your own hardware; may be ruled out only if the programme forbids personal equipment | ✔ |

**Recommendation — one primary, one fallback, the rest rejected.**

> **PRIMARY: Cisco Modeling Labs (CML)**, at the smallest topology that does the job — **one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall** (five devices, per **D-16**). It is the official Cisco virtualisation platform running genuine IOS-XE/IOS images, it gives real SSH, real SNMPv3 and real RESTCONF on images that support it, the topology is yours and persists, and it is the option a school is most likely to permit because it is a licensed Cisco product rather than a redistributed image. Confirm the licence, the image entitlement and the RAM cost in week 1 (**D-12**); that confirmation is the gate.

> **Where CML runs, stated explicitly, because it decides whether D-03 can be honoured.** CML runs on the **CML host** — its own machine, recorded in **B1** alongside the model host. The matrix row's own cost figure above is the reason this cannot be left implicit: CML needs **"~ 16–32 GB RAM for a small topology on top of the hypervisor"**, which is *in addition to* whatever that machine is already doing. Because **D-03 option (a) is mandatory — never co-locate the model with the 19-VM estate host** — the CML host must be checked against *both* rules before it is chosen: (i) it must not be the 19-VM estate host, and (ii) it must not be the model host unless the machine's RAM covers the model **and** CML's nodes **and** its own operating system with margin, which will usually rule co-location out. **B1 therefore captures two hosts, not one** (see §A), and §F's week-1 platform work is not complete until both are named.

> **FALLBACK: the hybrid — Packet Tracer for design plus 2–4 genuinely reachable devices** (either a small physical set, or DevNet Sandbox instances used as a supplement). This keeps the whole existing Packet Tracer design as the topology-fidelity and CCNA-level artefact **and** guarantees that the AI layer has at least a handful of devices it can truly manage. The only discipline it demands is labelling: every figure says either "simulated in Packet Tracer" or "a real device with a real management interface".

> **If the school's rules force a simulator-only environment**, see C.5 — the scope reduction is real and must be written down, not glossed.

**Rejection rationale.**

| Option | Verdict | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **(b) GNS3 with genuine IOS/IOS-XE images** | **Not recommended as the platform of record** | The software is free and the interfaces are real, but the **IOS images are the problem**: redistribution and use of IOS software outside licensed platforms is restricted, and sourcing "images for GNS3" from the community has no defensible answer for a graded deliverable. Use it only if the group is handed images under a licence it can cite. |
| **(c) EVE-NG** | **Not recommended** | Same image problem as GNS3, plus the heaviest local resource cost (a full VM per node) and a steeper administrative curve — which buys nothing this project needs that CML does not already provide. |
| **(d) Cisco DevNet Sandbox alone** | **Rejected as the environment of record; kept as a supplement** | Excellent for proving that the MCP tools drive a real, Cisco-hosted device. But reserved instances are **time-boxed and reset**, so the "network you built" is not an artefact you can hand in, re-run at marking time, or screenshot reproducibly. Using it as the primary environment would make the project's own verification results non-reproducible. |
| **(e) Physical second-hand gear alone** | **Rejected as the primary** | The strongest possible evidence, and genuinely the most honest option — but it needs a purchase with a lead time, it cannot host an ASA without buying one, and classic IOS has **no RESTCONF**, which removes one of the three management interfaces the project wants to demonstrate. It is an excellent **supplement** to CML or to the hybrid. |
| **Packet Tracer as the Cisco layer that the AI manages** | **Rejected outright — and this is the single biggest threat to the project's objective** | **Packet Tracer exposes no reachable SSH, no SNMP agent that a real poller can query, no NETCONF and no RESTCONF, and no API surface of any kind.** No MCP server, Ansible playbook, SNMP poller or AI agent can reach a Packet Tracer device. Any design that places the AI-managed Cisco layer inside Packet Tracer **cannot demonstrate AI network management at all**. Packet Tracer stays in the project as a **design-time** tool and nothing more. |

### C.5 If the school forces a simulator-only environment — the honest scope reduction

If D-12 fails, or if programme rules forbid a licensed platform, physical equipment and hosted sandboxes, then the Cisco layer cannot be AI-managed. Say so plainly and adjust the targets. **Do not** quietly keep the AI-management claim and hope nobody checks.

| Aspect | With a reachable Cisco layer | **Simulator-only fallback** |
|---|---|---|
| Cisco layer's role | A managed element of the estate | **Design-and-verify only.** Configured by hand in Packet Tracer, demonstrated by CLI and simulation-mode captures |
| AI management domains | **Three families**: Cisco, Linux, Windows | **Two families**: Linux and Windows only |
| MCP servers | Five, including `MCP-S-CISCO` | **Four** — `MCP-S-CISCO` is dropped, and the report states why in one sentence |
| Use cases | UC-1 triage spans three families | **UC-1 narrows to Linux + Windows.** UC-2 (firewall drops correlated with routing/ACL state) becomes **partly** demonstrable, using the Linux nftables pair and the Windows estate instead of the ASA |
| Evaluation | Fault injection across three families | Fault injection across two; the Cisco layer keeps its own (non-AI) verification chapter |
| Assessment consequence | The AI line is a genuine cross-domain management plane | The AI line becomes **"AI management of a heterogeneous server estate, with the Cisco domain designed and verified conventionally"** — still a defensible project, still topic-faithful on four of five brief lines, but **materially weaker on the fifth** |
| What must be written | — | A **scope-reduction note** in the proposal and the report's limitations chapter, naming the constraint, the option that was unavailable, and the assessment impact. **A stated reduction is an honest result; a hidden one is a fabrication** |
| Mitigation available | — | Push harder on the **paper trail**: the Cisco configuration chapter, the packet-flow analysis, and a written design for what the MCP-S-CISCO server *would* have exposed, with its tool signatures fully specified. Design work is a legitimate deliverable; a claimed implementation is not |

**One sentence for the report, to be reused verbatim:** *"Packet Tracer was used to design and demonstrate the topology and CCNA-level configuration; it was not, and cannot be, the platform on which the AI management plane operates, because its devices expose no reachable SSH, SNMP or API surface."*

### C.6 The staged AI-coverage ramp — how management is introduced incrementally (SQ-1's answer, made operational)

**Why this section exists.** SQ-1 is answered and the direction is ratified: the Cisco layer **must** become genuinely reachable and AI-manageable, and **Packet Tracer cannot be that layer**. The student's answer adds a qualification that this outline must carry rather than discard: 「期望係完全管理，可逐步加入，非一步到位」 — **full management is the destination, but it is introduced incrementally, not all at once**. This section is that increment, written as *stages with gates*. It introduces no new physics: every row it relies on already exists in the **normative reachability matrix at §C.2b**, and every week it names already exists in the **phased plan at §F**. What it adds is the **ordering relationship between the three things the project otherwise states separately** — and it makes that ordering a **hard dependency** rather than a schedule coincidence:

```
   (1) SERVICES UP  ──────►  (2) AI MANAGES Linux + Windows  ──────►  (3) AI MANAGES Cisco
        §D.3, §F.2               Stage A                                  Stage B, then Stage C
        G2, end of week 6        RW-01 … RW-06, RW-09                    RW-07, RW-08
                                        │
                                        └──────►  OPTIONAL  (4) PEER-SIDE BGP  —  Stage D
                                                  RW-07 second AS-65000 node (NR-01a today)
```

**Each arrow above is a hard dependency, not a preference:** no MCP server may be written before the thing it manages is reachable, authenticated and logging (§B.2's rule, and §L.5's gate). **No stage may be entered by declaring it entered — a stage is entered only by its entry gate being evidenced, and left only by its exit gate being evidenced.** Stages may overlap in calendar time; they may not be reordered.

| Stage | What becomes AI-manageable | Preconditions (entry gate) | The demonstration that proves it | Evidence required | Exit gate | Weeks it can fall in (12-week plan, §F) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A — the server estate** | The **Linux and Windows estate**: §C.2b **RW-01, RW-02, RW-03** (all 9 Windows guests), **RW-04, RW-05, RW-06** (all 10 Ubuntu guests) and **RW-09** (the agent host's own audit log). These hosts are already reachable, so this stage needs **no Cisco platform and no GPU** | (i) The named hosts are up, authenticated and producing telemetry — services first (§D.3); (ii) `MCP-S-LINUX`, `MCP-S-WIN`, `MCP-S-MON`, `MCP-S-DOC` are written against **only** rows evidenced `REACHABLE`; (iii) the tool allow-lists and the read-only/mutating classification are frozen | `MCP-S-LINUX`, `MCP-S-WIN`, `MCP-S-MON` and `MCP-S-DOC` answer real questions against the live estate; **UC-3 (drift check) and UC-6 (estate Q&A) run end to end**; the tool-calling probe set has been run and its malformed-call rate reported | One **filed transcript per `REACHABLE` row** (`Test-WSMan`, an allow-listed `ssh` command, a `systemctl show`, a `journalctl` query, a Prometheus target marked `up`); the `MCP-S-*` tool listings; the audit log; **§C.2b's `REACHABLE` rows filing their transcripts flip from claim to fact** (§C.2b's verification obligation) | Every row this stage claims is **evidenced in §C.2b and in §H.2 rule E9**, not merely designed; the AI layer answers questions about the two server families with transcripts | **Weeks 2–7.** Platform and templates in weeks 1–2, the server-estate services verified by **G2 (end of week 6)**, the AI-to-server demonstration in **week 7** when the AI line starts |
| **B — the first genuinely reachable Cisco device** | **One** Cisco device with a real management plane — **one node only, of the kind RW-07 will hold** (a CML IOS-XE node, or a genuinely reachable physical device). **RW-08 is not required to pass this stage** | (i) **G0** has ratified the platform (**B2**) and the lab rules permit it; (ii) `MCP-S-CISCO` is written against **one** device, behind an exec-level, `show`-only allow-list with **no configuration mode**; (iii) the device is logging to the central syslog store | A **read-only** end-to-end run on that single device: an `ssh` login from the management host succeeds, `show version` returns, one `snmpwalk -v3` returns data — **these three are the unconditional guarantee, and they are exactly what §B.3 C1 requires** — plus one `restconf` GET returning JSON **where the image supports it**. **The RESTCONF step is conditional, not part of the guarantee:** §C.1 and §B.3 C1 both state it that way, and a classic-IOS image that has no RESTCONF endpoint cannot fail C1 or this stage on that ground. The agent reports on the device **through `MCP-S-CISCO` only** | The exact `show version` string; the `snmpwalk -v3` output (authPriv; **SNMPv2c and community strings are forbidden**); syslog lines from that device in the central store; the device's §C.2b transcript; the `MCP-S-CISCO` tool listing | Every precondition and every piece of evidence is filed. **This is the minimum acceptable stage** (justified below): the exit gate proves the project can manage a genuine network device, in the design's own test discipline, on **one** device rather than on an aspiration | **Weeks 3–8.** `show version` capture **is** §F.1's **G1 (week 3)**, but the stage closes when the **AI** manages the device, which is **week 8** at the earliest because the AI line starts at week 7 (§L.5) |
| **C — the full Cisco target set** | The **five Cisco devices fixed by D-16** — one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall — held largely in **RW-07**, with **RW-08** carrying the ASA-class perimeter where that platform is available | **Stage B is exited.** The routing, switching and perimeter services those devices carry are up and verified (§D.1.3–§D.1.4) so that there is state for the agent to read | **UC-1 (cross-family fault triage) and UC-2 (firewall-drop correlation) run across all three families**; each of the five devices answers through `MCP-S-CISCO`; a device's own read-only surface is cited in a diagnosis | **Five §C.2b transcripts — one per device — under §C.2b's own rule that a `REACHABLE` row without a transcript is unverified**; the five-device topology; the `MCP-S-CISCO` allow-list as listed verbatim in the report (§E.3.3) | Every device the stage claims carries a transcript, and the count is reconciled with **D-16**. **Devices that sit inside Packet Tracer as `NR-01` are excluded by definition and must never be counted here** | **Weeks 8–10.** The device build runs weeks 3–6 and the routing protocols weeks 5–7; the AI-side closure is **G4 (week 10)** |
| **D — OPTIONAL: peer-side eBGP verification** | The **peer AS-65000 provider**, so that the BGP session can be verified from **both** sides. **This is impossible today:** the provider is `VP-ISP-EDGE-01`, a Packet Tracer object — **§C.2b row NR-01a** | (i) **Stage C is exited**; (ii) a **second genuinely reachable CML IOS-XE node** is configured as AS-65000 under **D-01**, with the **D-12** licence covering the additional node; (iii) it is added to the matrix as an **RW-07** row rather than left as `NR-01a`; (iv) the number of devices this adds is reconciled with **D-16** | A **two-sided** BGP verification: `show ip bgp summary` and `show ip route bgp` read from **both** autonomous systems, through `MCP-S-CISCO`, in one recorded run | Transcripts from **both** sides, plus the reconciliation that moves the provider out of `NR-01a` and into `RW-07`; the licence record for the extra node | **A bonus, not an exit criterion for the project.** Exiting it converts §C.2b's honest limitation ("verified from the local autonomous system only") into a two-sided result, and **that improvement is what the report must state** — nothing less and nothing more | **Weeks 10–12, and only if Stage C closed early.** Its entry gate is *"Stage C is exited"*, and Stage C closes at **G4 (week 10)** — so **Stage D cannot begin before week 10**, and the window is the three weeks that remain. It consumes CML RAM (the CML host's cost is set out at §C.4) and one more licence-entitled node, so it is taken up **only after Stage C is evidenced** |

**Hard dependencies, stated once so that no later writer reorders them.**

1. **Services → AI on the servers (Stage A).** No AI work starts before the services it reads exist (§B.2).
2. **Server estate manageable → the first Cisco device manageable (A → B).** `MCP-S-CISCO` is a *different integration domain* from the server estate: it is not a variation of `MCP-S-WIN`, and it cannot be stood up while the platform question (**B2**) is unanswered — which is exactly what §F.4 already calls *"the one dependency that must never be reordered"*.
3. **One Cisco device → the Cisco target set (B → C).** This is the 1→5 relationship, and it is monotone **only in one direction**: the count may rise from one to five as transcripts are filed, and **may never be raised by design intent** — **D-16** and §C.2b's normative rule are what forbid it.
4. **Cisco target set → peer-side verification (C → D).** Optional, last, and never a precondition of anything else.

**How the AI line's committed demonstrations map onto the stages.** This is the mapping the report should print, because it makes the minimum stage concrete rather than rhetorical:

| Committed demonstration (§E.7, D-06) | Earliest stage that can carry it | Why that stage and not an earlier one |
|---|---|---|
| **UC-3** — read-only drift / compliance check | **A** | It is a lookup against the `MCP-S-DOC` baseline over the server estate, and it is deterministic |
| **UC-6** — natural-language questions about the estate | **A** | Inventory and IP-plan questions answered from `MCP-S-DOC`; also the clearest place to see hallucinations |
| **UC-5** — operations report from collected telemetry | **A** | Built from the monitoring and logging plane; needs no Cisco device to be meaningful |
| **UC-1** — cross-family fault triage | **C** (a narrowed version is runnable at **B**) | Its whole claim is *cross-family*: with two families it is not the use case the report describes. At Stage B it is run in the narrowed form (Linux + Windows + one Cisco device) and **the narrowing must be written down as such** |
| **UC-2** — firewall-drop correlation with routing and ACL state | **C** | The ACL/object-group and routing input is Cisco-side. §C.5 already concedes it is only *partly* demonstrable without a Cisco layer — and §E.7's own note says the same for the Linux/Windows case |
| **UC-4** — approval-gated change | **A**, and additionally **tier-gated** | The write path is server-side; but it stays closed until E.8 item 4's hallucination check passes and the tier supports it (§E.7's UC-4 caveat) |

**The minimum acceptable stage is Stage B, stated and justified as follows.** Stage A alone — AI management of the Linux and Windows estate with the Cisco layer merely designed and verified by hand — is a coherent project and is honestly describable as such, but **the student's ratified answer to SQ-1 requires the Cisco layer to become genuinely manageable**, and the only thing that demonstrates a genuinely manageable network device is a **device the AI layer actually manages**. Stage B is the **first stage at which that is true**, and it is achievable with **one** device, **one** server and **no GPU**, because the GPU decision was deferred to G2-H in week 6 while the Stage B device work runs weeks 3–8. **So Stage B is the floor, not the target.** Stage C is the **operational target**; Stage D is a **bonus**. **Stages A to D are therefore attempts in priority order.** **The floor is a sufficient stage and not merely a permitted one:** §B.3 C6 carries a stage-based reduction that is symmetric with its tier-based one — where the project closes at Stage B, C6 and §F.1's G4 are met by the reduced committed set UC-3 and UC-6, which is exactly the pair Stage A exits on and which needs no Cisco device. **So a project that lands on Stage B satisfies the definition of done; a project that lands on Stage A does not, and §C.5's scope reduction applies.** If Stage B cannot be evidenced by week 8 — after §F.1's G1 fall-through and §C.4's option sequence have both been exercised — then §C.5's scope reduction applies, **and it applies as a stated result with its assessment consequences written down, never as a silent slide from "we manage three families" to "we manage two".**

**Where this sits relative to the 12 weeks, stated plainly.** Stage B is the floor and Stage C is the target, so the plan is **not obliged to reach Stage D inside the 12 weeks** — Stage D's entry gate is *"Stage C is exited"* at **G4 (week 10)**, so it falls in **weeks 10–12** at the earliest and depends on a second CML node (RAM and licence, §C.4/§D-12). §F.1's gate table now carries **G2-H** (the deferred hardware decision, week 6) alongside **G1** (week 3), **G2** (week 6) and **G3** (week 8, where Stage B closes), and **G4** (week 10, where Stage C's use-case demonstrations land). **§F.3's second Gantt band is drawn to the same week cells, so the gate markers and the stage bars can be read against these weeks directly.**

