## D. Layer 1 — the network and the network services (build FIRST)

### D.1 The full prioritised service list

`MUST` = the defensible minimum core for 12 weeks and 4 people. `SHOULD` = build it if the MUST core is green by end of week 6. `OPTIONAL` = stretch, only after everything else is verified and documented.

#### D.1.1 Identity, naming and time

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| AD DS (directory, Kerberos, LDAP) | One identity for every Windows host and every administrator; the WSOS Domain 4 line names ADDS explicitly. It also creates the **service identities** the AI tools will use, which is why it is first. | **MUST** | `vpnz-dc01`, `vpnz-dc02` (Windows Server) | `dcdiag /v`, `repadmin /replsummary`, `klist`, an ADUC screenshot of the OU tree |
| DNS (AD-integrated) | Name resolution for every device family; without it, nothing else is diagnosable | **MUST** | `vpnz-dc01/02` forward + reverse zones | `Resolve-DnsName`, `dig`, zone export; a deliberate negative lookup |
| DHCP | Address assignment for user/VPN segments | **MUST** (small scope set) | `vpnz-dc01/02`, relay on the gateways | `Get-DhcpServerv4Lease`, a client `ip a` |
| NTP | Time skew ≥ 5 minutes breaks Kerberos. This is also the **single most useful injected-fault scenario** for the AI evaluation. | **MUST** | `vpnz-dc01` (Windows hierarchy), `vpnz-mon01` (Linux source) | `w32tm /stripchart`, `chronyc sources`; skew deliberately injected and corrected |
| PKI (internal CA) | Certificates for the VPN, for TLS on the proxy, and for WinRM HTTPS. Without a CA there is no certificate-based VPN. | **MUST** (single-tier acceptable) | AD CS on `vpnz-dc01`; Easy-RSA on `vpnz-lnxra01` | `certutil -CAInfo`, an issued certificate on a client, Easy-RSA CRL |
| AD sites, subnets, OU structure, groups | Makes the multi-site story real and gives the agent a **structured inventory** to reason about | **SHOULD** | `vpnz-dc01` | Screenshots of Sites and Subnets and of the OU tree |

#### D.1.2 File, storage and application services

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FSRM quotas and file screening | Quota enforcement and executable blocking on a share; gives a concrete, verifiable policy the agent can audit | **SHOULD** | `vpnz-fs01` | `Get-FsrmQuota`, `Get-FsrmFileScreen`; an **actual** blocked write and an actual quota-exceeded capture |
| iSCSI block storage | A LUN presented over the network and mounted as a disk; satisfies the brief's storage line | **SHOULD** | Target on `vpnz-san01`; initiator on one platform guest | `Get-IscsiServerTarget`, `Get-IscsiSession`, `Get-Disk`; the mounted volume in Explorer |
| Web hosting + reverse proxy with TLS | A published service behind a proxy — the natural "is the business service up?" target for AI triage | **MUST** | Nginx on `vpnz-dmzrev01/02`; IIS or a static app tier behind it | `nginx -t`, `curl -kI https://<name>`, `openssl s_client`, access log |
| High availability of the proxy (Keepalived / VRRP) | Keeps the published service up when one node dies. VRRP is **named in the WSOS** Domain 3 line, and Keepalived implements VRRP — this is the strongest available bridge. | **MUST** | `vpnz-dmzrev01/02` VRRP pair, floating VIP | Kill the master; capture the VIP move, the failover time and `journalctl -u keepalived` |

#### D.1.3 Perimeter, remote access and segmentation

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Host firewall and segmentation (nftables) | Stateful filtering with logging at a chokepoint; **the drop log is one of the richest signals the AI layer will read** | **MUST** | `vpnz-nft01/02` | `nft list ruleset`, `nft monitor`, a denied flow visible in the log |
| Perimeter firewall / ACLs / NAT (ASA-class) | The DMZ boundary and the NAT edge; on the Cisco layer this is also a **RESTCONF/SSH target** | **MUST** | ASA-class device in CML / physical | `show running-config`, `show access-list`, a denied-flow capture, `show asp drop` where available |
| Certificate-based remote access VPN (IKEv2 on RRAS, or OpenVPN) | Satisfies the brief's remote-access line and the WSOS VPN line. **Pick one to build properly rather than two badly — that choice is decision D-15, and it must be recorded by end of week 5.** | **SHOULD** (`MUST` if 1 only) | RRAS IKEv2 on `vpnz-rras01` **or** OpenVPN on `vpnz-lnxra01` | `Get-VpnConnection`, the client's `ip route`, NPS/OpenVPN log. OpenVPN is the **named** WSOS technology; IKEv2/RRAS is **not** in the standard but **is** in the brief — D-15 recommends **OpenVPN** for standards alignment, and IKEv2 only if the Windows line needs depth |
| Site-to-site IPsec tunnel | Links the branch to HQ over the Cisco layer | **SHOULD** | ASA-class ↔ branch router | `show crypto ipsec sa`, tunnel up, a ping across it |
| WireGuard tunnel | Cheap, fast, and a second independent tunnel implementation | **OPTIONAL** | Linux guest pair | `wg show`, handshake, inner ping. Note the baseline's own finding: **a Cisco device cannot terminate WireGuard**, so do not design it that way |

#### D.1.4 Routing and switching (the Cisco layer — the AI-reachable one)

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **OSPF** (single area 0) | Interior routing between HQ and a branch; **named in the WSOS** | **MUST** | CML/physical routers | `show ip ospf neighbor`, `show ip route ospf`, a link-down convergence capture |
| **RSTP** | Loop-free layer-2 with an explicit root; the "access/aggregation/core switching" WSOS line | **MUST** | CML/physical switches | `show spanning-tree`, root bridge selection, a link-pull topology change |
| **VLANs, trunks, port security, DHCP snooping** | Segmentation and control-plane hygiene; also gives the agent a topology to reason about | **MUST** | CML/physical switches | `show vlan brief`, `show interfaces trunk`, a rogue-DHCP blocked capture |
| **HSRP or VRRP** on the layer-3 gateways | First-hop redundancy; **both are named in the WSOS line together with GLBP, LACP and PAgP** | **SHOULD** | Core switches / router pair | `show standby brief` or `show vrrp`, a failover capture |
| **EIGRP** (a second interior protocol) | Named in the WSOS in the same line as OSPF and BGP; a second protocol also makes a genuinely interesting AI correlation case | **SHOULD** | A second site or a "legacy" segment | `show ip eigrp topology`, `show ip protocols`, one **controlled** redistribution point with route tags |
| **eBGP** to a simulated provider | Named in the WSOS; demonstrates path selection and failover. **The provider is `VP-ISP-EDGE-01` (AS-65000) and it is a Packet Tracer object — matrix row `NR-01a`, NOT REACHABLE.** | **SHOULD** | Edge router (`VP-HQ-EDGE-01`, RW-07) ↔ simulated provider (`VP-ISP-EDGE-01`, **NR-01a**) | `show ip bgp summary`, `show ip route bgp`, a session-down failover — **all from the local AS only.** **Peer-side verification is not available and must never be claimed**: the provider exposes no SSH, SNMP, NETCONF, RESTCONF or API surface, so no MCP tool, agent or script can read its view of the session (§C.2b NR-01a). To obtain a peer-side view, make AS-65000 a second genuinely reachable CML IOS-XE node under D-01 |
| **LACP / EtherChannel** | Named in the WSOS redundancy line | **OPTIONAL** | Switch pair | `show etherchannel summary` |
| QoS, MPLS, SDN/controller-based management, VXLAN | Not named, and heavy | **OPTIONAL / excluded** | — | If attempted, must be actually configured or listed as future work |

#### D.1.5 Monitoring, logging and telemetry (this is Layer 1 output that Layer 2 consumes)

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Metrics collection (Prometheus-class) + dashboards | Numeric evidence, and a clean machine-readable interface for the AI tools | **MUST** | `vpnz-mon01` | `/targets` all UP, a dashboard screenshot, an alert fired and resolved |
| Host exporters (Linux + Windows) | Per-host CPU/RAM/disk/interface series | **MUST** | All guests | Exporter endpoints responding; series present |
| **SNMPv3 polling of the Cisco estate** | **The minimum viable way for the AI layer to see the Cisco devices.** SNMP is named in the WSOS Domain 3 tooling line. | **MUST** | Poller on `vpnz-mon01`; agent on every Cisco device | `snmpwalk -v3` output; authPriv only — **SNMPv2c and community strings are forbidden** |
| Centralised syslog | The single highest-value AI input: all three families writing to one queryable store | **MUST** | Receiver on `vpnz-mon01`; senders across the estate | Per-host log files present; a searchable query for a known event |
| NetFlow / flow export from Cisco | Traffic visibility; named in the WSOS tooling line, but licence- and image-dependent | **OPTIONAL** | Edge/switch export → collector | Flow records received, or an explicit "unavailable on this image" note |
| `tcpdump` / packet capture | Named in the WSOS; the ground truth for a "why is this flow failing" scenario | **SHOULD** | Any Linux host | A capture file plus its read-back |
| Configuration archive + Git | Change history; **also the agent's drift baseline** | **MUST** | `vpnz-mon01` (`/srv/tftp` + Git) | Timestamped config archive entries; `git log` |

#### D.1.6 Automation foundations (the bridge into Layer 2)

| Service | What it is / why needed | Priority | Host / platform | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ansible control node | The WSOS **requires** infrastructure-as-code and names Ansible; also the natural place to hold the Cisco IOS collection and inventory that the MCP server will reuse | **MUST** | `vpnz-mon01` | `ansible --version`, `ansible -m ping` across all three families, a playbook run with `changed=0` on the second run |
| PowerShell scripting | Named in the WSOS; drives the Windows management surface the AI tools call | **MUST** | Windows guests; authoring on a jump box | Scripts plus their logs; a second run with identical output |
| Bash scripting | Named in the WSOS | **MUST** | Linux guests | Scripts plus their logs |
| Git repository and review workflow | Named in the WSOS (`Git`), and the audit trail the report can cite | **MUST** | `vpnz-mon01` | `git log --oneline --graph`, a reviewed pull/merge record |
| Cisco configuration automation over SSH | **Only possible because the Cisco layer is reachable.** This is the direct replacement for the old, unexecutable "two-track" approach. | **MUST** (given D-01) | `vpnz-mon01` → Cisco devices | Ansible `cisco.ios` (or equivalent) playbook run; a captured config change and an idempotent re-run |

### D.2 The defensible minimum core

If everything goes wrong except the MUST list, the project is still a coherent, verifiable enterprise network:

> **MUST core = AD DS + DNS + DHCP + NTP + internal CA; Nginx + Keepalived published service; nftables host firewall; ASA-class perimeter with ACL/NAT; OSPF + RSTP + VLANs + trunks; SNMPv3 + syslog + metrics collection; Ansible + Git + PowerShell + Bash.**
>
> That is a subset of the **19-VM estate the baseline's §6/§15.1 inventory fixes and this outline carries forward in full — 10 Ubuntu guests, 9 Windows Server guests and the hypervisor host** — plus the **five Cisco devices fixed as the target by D-16** (eight at most). The MUST core does not need every one of the 19 guests running at once; it needs every one of them **accounted for** in §C.2b, whether as `REACHABLE` or as a declared out-of-scope row. The estate fits a 12-week, four-person schedule with documentation time built in, and it is **entirely sufficient to support the AI layer**, because it yields all four signal classes the MCP servers need: reachable SSH/SNMP command surfaces, structured metrics, a central log store, and version-controlled configuration baselines.

### D.3 The staging order inside Layer 1

1. **Weeks 1–3** — platform decided and installed; host-only networks; templates; AD DS / DNS / DHCP / NTP / CA; the Cisco platform reachable at all (SSH + SNMP on one device is the week-3 gate).
2. **Weeks 4–6** — the application services (Nginx + Keepalived, file/storage, remote access), the perimeter (nftables + ASA), monitoring and syslog, and the Cisco L2/L3 build (VLANs, trunks, RSTP, OSPF).
3. **Weeks 7–8** — the remaining routing depth (EIGRP, eBGP, HSRP/VRRP), the configuration archive, and the automation foundations hardened to idempotent.

**Gate at end of week 6:** every MUST service is up and verified, and the AI feasibility spike decision is recorded. Nothing new is added after this gate except the AI layer and the SHOULD/OPTIONAL items already planned.

