**How to read the ruler, so that the chart and §F.1 cannot be read against each other.** Each week occupies a **five-character cell**: `W1` occupies columns 17–21, `W2` columns 22–26, and so on, `W12` occupying columns 72–76. **Every gate marker is drawn starting at its own week's column** — `G1` at column 27 (week 3), `G2` at column 42 (week 6), `G3` at column 52 (week 8) — and **no bar extends past column 76**, the right edge of the `W12` cell.

The same chart with **§C.6's staged AI-coverage ramp** and the **G2-H hardware gate** laid on top. The ramp is what the first chart cannot show — it is coverage growing in evidenced steps, not another bar — so it is drawn as a second band beneath the same week ruler rather than folded into the first:

```
                 W1   W2   W3   W4   W5   W6   W7   W8   W9   W10  W11  W12
                           G1             G2        G3        G4
                                          G2-H
STAGE A  (Linux+Windows AI-managed)..............................
STAGE B  (first Cisco device, AI-managed) ##########
STAGE C  (five Cisco devices, AI-managed)           ##########
STAGE D  (optional: peer-side eBGP, both AS)                  ..........
```

**The same alignment rule applies to this band, and it is what makes the ramp readable against §C.6.** **`G2-H` starts at column 42 and so sits directly beneath `W6`** (the deferred hardware gate, §F.1); **`G4` starts at column 62 and sits beneath `W10`** (where Stage C's use-case demonstrations land). **Each stage bar stops immediately before the column of the gate its stage closes at** — `STAGE B` occupies weeks 6–7 and ends at column 51, one column left of `G3` (week 8, where Stage B closes); `STAGE C` occupies weeks 8–9 and ends at column 61, one column left of `G4` (week 10, where Stage C closes) — **so the bars no longer sit to the right of the gates they close at, and `STAGE D` stops inside the `W11` cell rather than running past the `W12` edge.** `STAGE A` spans weeks 2–7, and the `STAGE D` bar is drawn in weeks 10–11 because **§C.6 now places Stage D in weeks 10–12** — it cannot start before Stage C is exited at G4.

### F.4 Parallelism and hard dependencies

| Can run fully in parallel | Hard dependency (blocks) |
|---|---|
| AD DS/DNS (S2) ∥ host templates (S2/S3) ∥ Cisco platform bring-up (S1) ∥ repository setup (S4) | Everything depends on the hypervisor and the platform decision (G0) |
| Nginx/Keepalived (S3) ∥ Cisco L2/L3 (S1) ∥ FSRM/iSCSI (S2) | Both depend on AD DS + DNS |
| Ansible inventory (S4) ∥ all build work | Ansible needs at least one reachable Linux host and one reachable Windows host |
| **MCP-S-LINUX** (S4) ∥ **MCP-S-WIN** (S2/S4) ∥ **MCP-S-CISCO** (S1/S4) | Each blocks on its own family being reachable **and logging** — hence the strict "services first" order |
| Evaluation harness (S4) ∥ verification records (all) | Both need the MCP contract frozen (end of week 7) |

**The one dependency that must never be reordered:** MCP-S-CISCO cannot start until G1 passes. If the Cisco platform decision slips past week 4, the AI layer loses one of its three families and the project's differentiating claim weakens accordingly — which is exactly why D-01 is a week-1 decision.

**The second dependency, and the one SQ-2 created (made explicit at §C.6 rather than left implicit).** The **network and services layer, the MCP servers, the agent scaffolding and the evaluation harness are all independent of the hardware decision** (§E.1.1a) and must be worked on continuously from week 1, **including across weeks 1–6 when the model tier is still unknown**. The **model class and the serving configuration** are the only things that wait, and they wait until **G2-H at the end of week 6** — a gate that falls one week before the AI line starts in week 7, so **the deferral costs the schedule nothing**. **The failure mode this guards against is a group that treats B1's deferral as permission to idle on the AI line**: the correct response to "the machine is not decided yet" is to build the tool surface, the guardrails and the evaluation instruments, all of which are hardware-agnostic, and to let the ~20-probe measurement at G2-H choose the model. **§C.6's ramp is the same ordering seen from the AI side**: Stage A (the server estate) needs no Cisco platform and no GPU; Stage B needs one reachable Cisco device; only the model tier waits.

