**The two submission tiers, stated here as well as at §H.3, because the deliverable list is where a reader looks first.** **(i) The immediate tier is the proposal (D5) — nothing else is being submitted at this stage.** **(ii) The later tier is the full document at the 90,000–110,000-word scale treated at §H.3**, into which D6–D12 are assembled. An **initial report** is **not** current scope: it is recorded at §H.3 as a **likely future deliverable** that follows the proposal in the programme's normal sequence, with its structure `[TO CONFIRM]` against the programme handbook.

### H.2 The evidence standard (the topic sheet demands it; it is where marks are won and lost)

The topic sheet requires *"all findings, manuals, steps, screen captures, verifications, and references"*. WSOS 2026 does **not** mention screen captures at all, so attribute the requirement correctly: it is the **HDTN brief's**, not the standard's.

| Rule | Requirement |
|---|---|
| E1 | **Every figure is captioned** in the form `Figure {n}: {what} — {host} — {command or action} — {date}`. An uncaptioned screenshot is treated as absent. |
| E2 | **Every claim of "it works" is backed by two things:** a command transcript (text) *and* a captioned screenshot (image). Text alone is weakly evidenced; an image alone cannot be searched. |
| E3 | **Filenames are mechanical:** `{date}_{host}_{what}.png`, for example `2026-10-06_vpnz-dc01_dcdiag.png`. Never `screenshot1.png`. |
| E4 | **Dated and versioned.** The capture must show enough context to prove *which build* produced it — a version banner, a prompt with the hostname, or a visible date. |
| E5 | **Reproducible.** Every verification record names the exact command and the exact expected result, so a reader can re-run it. |
| E6 | **Honest about failure.** A test that failed or was not run says so. A fault deliberately injected and then reverted is documented with both the injection and the revert. |
| E7 | **AI-generated text is labelled.** If any prose was drafted with a model, the methodology chapter says so, and **no technical result may rest on generated text** — results come from transcripts and captures. |
| E8 | **No invented version, URL or licence.** Every version string is captured from the running system; every URL is copied from a browser and archived; anything unconfirmed is marked `[TO CONFIRM]` in the draft and resolved before submission. |
| E9 | **Reachability is evidenced, not asserted.** For every `REACHABLE` row in the Section C.2b matrix there is a transcript showing that interface returning data from that device. A reachability claim with no transcript is listed in the report as **unverified** |

### H.3 Submission size and shape — the two submission tiers (SQ-5, ANSWERED)

**SQ-5 is answered, and the answer is short: the immediate submission is the proposal alone** — the student's words are 「暫時只做 proposal」. Everything below is therefore arranged around **two tiers** rather than one, and **the immediate tier is the tier this project is currently working to**.

| Tier | What it is | Size expectation | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| **(i) IMMEDIATE — the proposal** | The proposal is the artefact being submitted now. **Nothing else is being submitted at this stage**, and no other document should be planned as if it were | **An FYP proposal's own band** — stated by the brief as **15–25 pages** (§H.1 D5). The programme's own word band for the proposal is `[TO CONFIRM]` against the programme handbook, and this outline does not invent one | **Current target.** D5 in §H.1 |
| **(ii) LATER — the full submission** | The full document, assembled from D6–D12, into which the network build lines, the automation and verification material and the AI / MCP line are written up at the 90,000–110,000-word scale | **90,000–110,000 words**, hard ceiling 120,000 — a **58–69% cut** from the existing 286,932-word corpus | **Later, and not the immediate deliverable.** The figures in the table below remain recommendations **for that later tier** |

**A likely future deliverable, recorded in one line and NOT current scope.** An **initial report** is expected to follow the proposal in the programme's normal sequence — introduction and background; aims and objectives; literature and technology review; proposed methodology and design; implementation plan; risks; references — but **its exact required structure is `[TO CONFIRM]` against the programme handbook and is not invented here**, and it is **not** work to be done at this stage. *(An earlier answer to SQ-5 suggested completing an initial report alongside the proposal; that was narrowed by the student to 「暫時只做 proposal」, and the narrowed answer is the one recorded here.)*

**The size comparison, made against the immediate tier rather than only against the full book.** The immediate tier is graded on the **proposal**, and the proposal is not where the 90,000–110,000-word question is settled. Two consequences follow, and they are the reason this section was rewritten: **(a)** the figures below are **planning targets for the later tier**, not a description of what is being handed in now; and **(b)** **this outline's own length — see the measured note immediately below — does not sit inside the immediate tier's band, and it is not meant to.** The outline is a **working reference artefact** (it doubles as the reachability register, the change record and the decision log), so its overshoot is a property of *this document*, not of the proposal that will be submitted. **The thing that must be normalised for the immediate tier is the proposal's length, and the proposal is a separate, shorter artefact (D5, 15–25 pages).**

The existing corpus is **286,932 words** across eight sections, and the gap analysis judges it to be two to three times what this project should carry. The figures below are the **later tier's** recommended shape, and **they are recommendations for the supervisor to confirm, not unilateral decisions**.

| Deliverable | Recommended target | Change from the existing corpus |
|---|---|---|
| **The proposal (immediate tier)** | **15–25 pages** (D5); the programme's own word band is `[TO CONFIRM]` | Was not previously a distinct artefact of this shape |
| **This outline** | **A working reference artefact, not a banded deliverable.** Its measured size is stated honestly in the note below; it sits **well above** the 6,000–10,000-word outline band because it carries the reachability register, the withdrawal register and the decision log | Was not previously a distinct artefact |
| **Focus-corrected full proposal (later tier)** | **90,000–110,000 words**, hard ceiling 120,000 | A **58–69% cut** from 286,932 |
| — front matter, aim, objectives, scope, standards alignment, gap disclosure | ~6,000 words | Merged from `01` §1–§4 |
| — the three network build lines (Microsoft, Linux, Cisco) | ~45,000 words | Down from 111,219, compressed but otherwise kept |
| — automation line and verification/testing | ~15,000–18,000 words | Testing/verification methods are the corpus's strongest asset; keep the method, compress the prose |
| — **the AI / local-model + MCP line (the differentiator)** | **~18,000 words** | **Up** in prominence from 21,202 words of mainly-OpenAI-runtime-independent text, re-based onto model serving + MCP |
| — implementation schedule, WBS, RACI, cut bands | ~6,000 words | Down from 66,754 words |
| — documentation, evidence and risk management | ~6,000 words | Compressed |

**Note on this outline's own size — and the ONE counting method this document uses from here on.** The initial brief asked for 4,000–7,000 words; the orchestrating agent has since confirmed the outline band is **6,000–10,000 words**. An earlier draft of this note quoted *"approximately 19,500 words by a prose count that excludes fenced code blocks (~18,650 Latin words plus ~1,350 Chinese characters)"* and *"22,394 whitespace-split words"*. **Those figures could not be reproduced by any method, and they are superseded.** The reason they could not be reproduced is that the document never said which method it was using; it therefore mixed a *prose* count with a *converter* count and understated both.

**The canonical method, stated so that anyone can re-run it:**

1. **Render the Markdown to `.docx` with `proposal\tools\md2docx\convert.ps1`.** The `.docx` is the artefact; its text is what a reader actually sees, so the `.docx` is the unit of measurement.
2. **Extract the visible text by raw ZIP access** — read `word/document.xml` from the package (Node `zlib`, or .NET `System.IO.Compression.ZipFile` + `XmlDocument`), take the content of every `w:t` node, concatenate the runs **within** each paragraph, and drop `<w:instrText>`/`<w:delText>` field plumbing. **Never open the `.docx` with the Open XML SDK by path, Word COM or Word** — all three can rewrite the file in place.
3. **`whitespace-split words` = every maximal run of non-whitespace characters in that extracted text**, over every paragraph including table cells and code-block lines. This is the same measure `validate.ps1` reports as *"extracted words (whitespace split)"*, so the figure in this document and the figure in the validation report are the **same number, from the same tool** — that is the whole point of naming the method.
4. **`CJK characters` = count of `[\u4E00-\u9FFF]` code points** in the same extracted text, counted **per character** (CJK has no whitespace word boundaries, which is why it needs its own measure).
5. **Anything else — a Latin-only count, a "prose" count excluding fenced blocks or table markup — is a derived figure and must say so.** The Latin token count is not used as a headline figure anywhere in this outline.
6. **The heading claim, stated literally so that it cannot be read as saying something it does not.** This document **does contain one `# ` H1 heading** — line 1, `# Victor2 – Enterprise Network Infrastructure and Architect`, the document title. What is true, and what any earlier shorthand about "no H1" was reaching for, is narrower than that shorthand: **(i) there is exactly one H1 and it is the title — no body section of this outline uses H1** (the shallowest body heading is `## `, level 2); and **(ii) the converter renders that one title with the `TitlePageTitle` paragraph style rather than `Heading1`, so the delivered `.docx` contains no `Heading1`-styled paragraph at all**, while `validate.ps1`'s comparison still reconciles the two sides by subtracting the title-styled paragraph (`markdown 1 (minus 1 title-styled) vs document 0`). **The count is therefore H1 1 / H2 15 / H3 73 / H4 12 in the Markdown, and the render's `TitlePageTitle` 1 / `Heading2` 15 / `Heading3` 73 / `Heading4` 12 matches it level for level.**

**The measured figures under that method, after the fixes and this revision recorded in `proposal\integration\outline-fix-log.md`.** The counts below are the delivered render's own figures, and **inserting this table and the conclusion beneath it changes the document by roughly the length of the text you are reading** — which is why the figure is reported as a measurement with its method rather than as a constant.

| Measure | Value | How it was produced |
|---|---|---|
| **whitespace-split words (canonical)** | **37,555** | `convert.ps1` output, then `word/document.xml` `w:t` extraction, whitespace split — the identical measure `validate.ps1` prints as *"extracted words (whitespace split)"*, and the two agree on the delivered file |
| **CJK characters** | **3,253** | same extracted text, `[\u4E00-\u9FFF]`, per character |
| Latin (non-CJK) whitespace words | 34,302 | derived figure — canonical count minus the CJK characters, stated as such (§H.3 rule 5). **Not used as a headline figure** |
| Markdown source, whitespace-split | 40,540 | the raw `.md`; **different** from the canonical figure because the source still carries table pipes, list markers, emphasis syntax and blank lines that the renderer drops. **Reported for transparency only — the `.docx` figure governs** |

**The honest conclusion, restated for the two-tier situation and now resting on a stated method rather than on a method nobody could reproduce.** This document sits **well above** the 6,000–10,000-word outline band — the figures above are the measurement, and the earlier *"~19,500 words / 22,394 whitespace words"* understated it by around **48% and 42%** respectively. At **37,555** whitespace-split words it is roughly **3.8× the band's ceiling**, and **the growth is deliberate and attributable**: an earlier revision added the staged AI-coverage ramp (§C.6), the hardware-agnostic first phase (§E.1.1a) and the two submission tiers (§H.3), and expanded the Chinese executive summary to match the English — and this revision added the consistency reconciliation of §A/§B/§C/§F/§J/§K/§L, which is why the canonical figure moved from **35,384** to **37,555**. **The figure is re-measured from the delivered render at the end of every revision, because inserting this table and this conclusion changes the document by roughly the length of the text you are reading — which is why it is reported as a measurement with its method rather than as a constant.** **That overshoot is a property of the working reference artefact and not of the immediate submission:** the immediate tier is the **proposal (D5, 15–25 pages)**, which is a separate, shorter artefact, and the 90,000–110,000-word figures apply to the **later tier**. **So the overshoot has one operational consequence, and it is the one option 1 below already recommends: the proposal must be written from the decision material rather than by pruning this document.** The three options below therefore stand, and option 1 is still the recommendation for the immediate tier.
1. **Produce a condensed 6,000–10,000-word decision brief for the supervisor meeting**, drawn from this document — **and this is now the recommended route to the immediate tier's own artefact, the proposal (D5)**. Sections **A** (the decision table, A.1's five supervisor questions and their four answers), **C.2a–C.2b** (the reachability rule and matrix), **C.4–C.6** (the Cisco recommendation, the scope reduction, and the staged AI-coverage ramp), **E.1.1–E.1.2** (the hardware-agnostic phase and the tiered model table) and **M** (next steps) alone carry the decision content — roughly a third of this document.
2. **Treat this document as the working outline and the reference**, and accept the overshoot, noting in the proposal's methodology chapter that the outline exceeded the recommended band and why (it doubles as the reachability register and the change record).
3. **Cut this document down** to the band, archiving the withdrawn-statement register (L.4) and the reachability matrix (C.2b) as separate working papers.

**Recommendation: option 1**, with option 2 as the fallback if time is short. A supervisor should be able to accept or reject the direction in fifteen minutes; the decision content is concentrated in the sections listed above, and the remainder is reference material the group needs only once the direction is agreed.

