## I. Standards alignment (WSOS 2026, Skill 2447)

### I.1 The six assessment domains and this project's emphasis

| # | Domain (exact title) | Weight | This project's coverage | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Work organization and management | 5% | **Covered.** Planning, scheduling, prioritisation, methodical work, research, and the innovation justification. | Sections A, F, G, K; the decision log; the meeting minutes |
| 2 | Communication and interpersonal Skills | 5% | **Covered indirectly**, through the documentation set, the RACI split, the cross-cover exercise and the sign-off records. | Sections G, H; the runbook-execution records |
| 3 | Data Transfer Networks | 25% | **Strongest coverage.** OSPF, EIGRP, BGP, HSRP/VRRP, LACP, VLANs, IPv4 addressing, VPN/IPsec, CDP/LLDP, SNMP, Syslog, NetFlow, `tcpdump`, access/aggregation/core switching. | Sections C, D.1.3–D.1.5 |
| 4 | Network and System Operations | 25% | **Strong coverage.** ADDS, DNS, DHCP, NAT, NTP, remote access (SSH/RDP), PKI (AD CS + Easy-RSA), sharing services (SMB/DFS), web servers (Nginx/IIS), backup, monitoring systems, file systems. | Section D.1.1–D.1.2 |
| 5 | Infrastructure Automation | 15% | **Strong coverage.** Bash, PowerShell, Git, Ansible, IaC, and the CI/CD *concept* documented. | Section D.1.6, plus the whole AI plane as an automation consumer |
| 6 | Troubleshooting | 25% | **Strong coverage.** The verification chapter, the injected-fault set, `tcpdump`/log-based diagnosis, and the requirement to prepare a technical document "for agreement and sign-off". | Sections D, E.8, H |

**Consequence for the group's effort allocation:** the three 25% domains are 75% of the standard. The network build and the troubleshooting/verification work are therefore **not overhead around the AI project**; they are the bulk of the assessment. The AI layer is the differentiator, not the core.

### I.2 Named in the standard (citable verbatim) — and used here

ADDS, LDAP, Windows DNS, BIND, DHCP/DNS/NAT/NTP (in words), SSH, Remote Desktop Services, AD CS, OpenSSL, SMB, DFS, NFS, FTP, Apache, **Nginx**, **IIS**, `Windows Server Backup`, `rsync`, WDS, Group Policy, Software RAID, `mdadm`, LVM, NTFS, ReFS, EXT4, **OSPF, BGP, EIGRP**, GLBP, **HSRP, VRRP**, **LACP**, PAgP, IPSec, SSL VPN, Direct Access, **OpenVPN**, DMVPN, GRE, **CDP, NetFlow, Syslog, SNMP, tcpdump**, VLAN, IPv4, IPv6, **Bash, PowerShell, Git, Ansible**, Icinga2/Nagios/Cacti/Windows Resource Monitor, Cisco/Microsoft/Linux certification.

### I.3 Named in the topic sheet but **ABSENT from the standard** — justify from the brief, not from the standard

**FSRM, iSCSI, RRAS, IKEv2, Keepalived, nftables, WireGuard, RSTP, ASA.** Each has **zero occurrences** in the 2026 WSOS text *and* in its raw HTML. Legal bridges (present them as bridges, quoting the standard line and then stating the inference):

| Absent technology | Legal bridge in the standard |
|---|---|
| Keepalived | The Domain 3 line names **VRRP**; Keepalived implements VRRP. *This is the strongest bridge available.* |
| nftables | `Apply basic security configuration for control and data plane` (D3) |
| RSTP | `Configure access, aggregation and core level switching` (D3) |
| WireGuard | Only the `etc.` of the D3 VPN line — **the weakest bridge; say so** |
| ASA, IKEv2, RRAS | The D3 VPN line and the D3 control/data-plane security line |
| FSRM, iSCSI | `Install and manage sharing services` and `Manage file systems` (D4) |

### I.4 The AI line — innovation **justified by**, but **beyond**, the standard

**The fact, stated without hedging: WSOS 2026 contains no AI content whatsoever.** Zero occurrences of `AI` (every apparent hit is a substring of *ShanghAI*, *dAIly*, *mAIntain*, *domAIn*), of `artificial intelligence`, of `machine learning`, of `Agentic`, of `OpenClaw`, and of `MCP` / `Model Context Protocol`. **No claim that the standard covers agentic AI or MCP is supportable, and making one would be trivially contradicted.**

The standard does, however, require the professional attributes that this work satisfies — all from Domain 1:

> `The speed of IT systems change and the need to maintain currency.` (D1, know/understand)

> `Use thorough and efficient research methods to support knowledge growth` (D1, shall be able to)

> `Be enthusiastic to try new methods, systems, and embrace change` (D1, shall be able to)

**The framing the report must use:** *"The standard requires maintaining currency with a fast-changing industry. Agentic AI network management is the current frontier of that change; the MCP-based management plane is this project's exercise of the research, currency and embrace-change requirements in Domain 1 — an innovation justified by the standard and deliberately beyond it. It is not claimed as WSOS compliance."*

**Where the differentiator sits relative to the standard, stated so the two claims cannot be confused (SQ-4, answered yes).** The **AI / MCP layer is the project's assessed differentiator**, and the **network is the foundation it depends on** — the arrangement §I.1's effort allocation already implies (the three 25% domains are the bulk of the assessment; the AI layer is the differentiator, not the core) and that §I.6 and §I.7 state in their own terms. **This does not move a single word of the standard, and the argument above must not be weakened to accommodate it.** The honest position has three parts, and all three must appear together in the report: **(i)** the AI content is **outside** the standard — WSOS 2026 contains no AI content whatsoever, and no occurrence of `MCP` or `Model Context Protocol`; **(ii)** it is nevertheless **justified by** the standard's own Domain 1 currency, research and embrace-change requirements, quoted verbatim above; and **(iii)** it is **not claimed as WSOS compliance** — the standards-aligned coverage of this project is the network, the services, the automation and the documentation, exactly as §I.1–§I.3, §I.5 and §I.7 set out. **A differentiator that is argued honestly from the brief is stronger than one smuggled into a standard that does not contain it**, and that is the only version of the claim this outline permits.

### I.5 The 2026 edition removed Python, YANG, RESTCONF and NETCONF

All four were present in the 2024 Domain 5 text and are **absent from 2026**. Therefore: **do not cite the standard as requiring RESTCONF or NETCONF.** This matters because this project deliberately *uses* RESTCONF as a management interface — a legitimate engineering choice justified by the brief's AI-management line, by the need for a structured device API, and by the topic sheet's own earlier MCP wording — but **not** by the standard. Say exactly that.

### I.6 Honest scope: what falls **outside** the standard

| Outside the standard | Why it is still in the project |
|---|---|
| **All AI content** — the local model, MCP, the agent, the guardrails, the evaluation | The topic sheet names it; it is the project's differentiator |
| FSRM, iSCSI, RRAS, IKEv2, Keepalived, nftables, WireGuard, RSTP, ASA | Named by the topic sheet |
| The simulator/emulator platform choice (Packet Tracer, CML, GNS3, EVE-NG) | A project constraint; the standard names no simulation tool |
| The **screen-capture** evidence requirement | **The HDTN brief's**, not the standard's — the standard says `screen`, `capture`, `screenshot`, `evidence` zero times |
| The **12-week** duration | A student constraint; the standard contains no time limit and no module structure |
| Docker/Kubernetes/Terraform/Puppet/Chef/Jenkins/CI-CD pipelines as *built* artefacts | Not named; documented as concepts and future work only |

### I.7 What the standard *does* require, and would be a mistake to omit

> **`Prepare a technical document reflecting the solution in detail for agreement and sign-off`** (Domain 6, 25%)

This single line justifies the entire documentation deliverable, and it sits inside a **25%-weighted** domain. Together with `The purpose of documenting the completion of the installation or up-grade` and `Gain user acceptance and record`, it makes the documentation set — not the AI demo — the highest-certainty source of marks in this project. **Plan the documentation as a first-class deliverable from week 1, not as week-12 tidying.**

### I.8 Read-only-by-default with approved changes — why this is a strength, not a limitation

The project's permission model (read-only by default; a change may happen only after a human approves a fully specified proposal, with an immutable audit log) is sometimes presented by students as a *compromise* — as if a bolder project would let the agent act alone. **It is not a compromise. It is the professional standard of practice, and it is where this project is strongest against the WSOS.** Four reasons, all of which belong in the report's rationale:

| # | Argument | Standard hook |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Least privilege and accountable change are exactly what the standard's Domain 1 and Domain 6 requirements describe.** The agent holds a minimum-rights service identity; every change has a named human approver; every action is documented. | `The importance of integrity and security when dealing with user equipment and information` (D1); `The importance of methodical working practices` (D1); `The purpose of documenting the completion of the installation or up-grade` (D6) |
| 2 | **The proposal-and-sign-off workflow is the standard's own language.** "Produce a change proposal → obtain agreement → execute → record acceptance" is a direct instance of the instruction the standard gives for technical work. | **`Prepare a technical document reflecting the solution in detail for agreement and sign-off`** and `Discuss the proposed solution(s) for role/feature and agree with relevant parties` and `Gain user acceptance and record` (all D6, a 25%-weighted domain) |
| 3 | **Safety and integrity: the project's own verification results stay reproducible.** An unsupervised agent writing to a router or a domain controller would make every earlier test result invalid, which is an academic-integrity problem as much as an engineering one. | `The importance of safe and secure disposal of waste…` and `The significance of accuracy, checking, and attention to detail in all working practices` (D1) |
| 4 | **It demonstrates currency.** Read-only-default agent design with human-in-the-loop approval is how the industry is actually deploying agentic tooling against live estates today. A project that adopted the reckless version would be demonstrating an outdated idea. | `The speed of IT systems change and the need to maintain currency.` (D1) — the same line that justifies the AI line at all (see I.4) |

**The sentence for the report's rationale chapter:** *"The agent operates read-only by default and executes a change only after a human has approved a fully specified proposal — target, exact commands or diff, expected effect, rollback plan and blast radius — with every prompt, tool call, result and approval recorded in a tamper-evident audit log. This is not a reduction of the project's ambition; it is the deployment model that professional practice requires for an agent with access to infrastructure, and it is demonstrated and tested (tests PB-1 … PB-9) rather than asserted."*

**The one thing this policy does forbid, stated honestly:** an impressive-looking "the agent fixed it by itself" demonstration. That demonstration would be worth less academically than the refusal tests, because a system that cannot be shown to refuse has not been evaluated at all.

