| **Options (tier)** | **T0** CPU only; **T1** ~8 GB VRAM; **T2** ~12–16 GB VRAM; **T3** 24 GB+; plus: is the model served there while the estate lives elsewhere, or both on one machine? |
| **CLOSED already** | **Hardware is available in principle** — school/laboratory machines may be used, so the "one student laptop only" constraint is relaxed for compute. |
| **RECORDED ACTION (blocking at a scheduled gate — the timing changed with SQ-2)** | **Capture and write down: GPU model; exact VRAM in GB; system RAM; free disk; CPU and core count; OS and version; whether the machine is shared, bookable or dedicated for 12 weeks; what the lab permits installing; and whether outbound network access is permitted and to where.** Owner: **S4**. **The gate is G2-H, end of week 6** (was "end of week 1"). **Why the date moved and not the requirement:** SQ-2's answer is 「未定，但應有硬件資源，需要待我返港才可安排」 — the resources are expected to exist, but the machine and GPU will be arranged on the student's return to Hong Kong. **A deferral is not a refusal: the same fields are captured, in the same detail, at G2-H — before the AI line's model work begins in week 7.** **This is the note that makes the deferral safe rather than circular: the model choice is blocked on this record, and the record is owed by a dated gate with a named owner.** |
| **What the deferral does NOT block (new — §E.1.1a)** | **The network and services layer (the whole of §D); the five MCP servers and their tests; the agent scaffolding, the approval gate and the guardrails; and the evaluation harness.** None of these needs a GPU, a VRAM figure or a model class, and all are scheduled in weeks 1–7 regardless. **Only the model class and the serving configuration wait.** |
| **Recommendation** | **T2 (~12–16 GB VRAM) as the target**, with the model served on the lab machine and the estate hosted elsewhere (**option (a)** — never co-locate the model with the 19-VM estate host). T1 is a workable floor; T0 is survivable but narrows the use cases, as set out in E.1.3 |
| **If the specification is still unknown at G2-H (week 6)** | Do not pick a model on reputation and do not idle. **Take the T0/T1 plan, run the tool-calling probe set against two or three small candidates, and let the measured malformed-call rate choose the model** — the probe set is hardware-agnostic and is built in weeks 1–6 as part of §E.1.1a. The AI line proceeds on the fallback and **says so in the report** |
| **Consequence to state in the report** | The tier determines the achievable use cases, not the other way round. **Choose the hardware, then measure the model, then commit the use cases — in that order.** |
| **By when** | **G2-H, end of week 6** (specification recorded — B1; tier selected — D-04; the week-5–6 spike's measured malformed-call rate names the model); **confirmed at G3 (week 8)**. **There is no longer any "provisional end of week 3" date: B3 cannot be settled before the hardware record exists** |

### D-13 — Offline-only model, or hybrid access?

| | |
|---|---|
| **Decision** | Must the model be strictly local/offline, or is a hosted fallback acceptable? |
| **Status** | **OPEN — this row is not closed, and it is one of the items §M item 6 asks the group to answer.** It is deliberately absent from §A's list of the three CLOSED rows (D-02, D-07, D-11). The recommendation below is firm, but a recommendation is not a decision |
| **Options** | (a) strictly local/offline only; (b) local primary + a hosted non-OpenAI-class fallback for the emergency case |
| **Recommendation** | **(a) as the delivered, described and demonstrated configuration. (b) only as a documented contingency**, named in the risk register, with its own limitation note, and **never** used for a scenario whose result appears in the evaluation chapter |
| **Consequence of (b) as the primary** | It weakens the "本地" requirement, sends device configuration and log content to a third party (a bad look in a project about least privilege), and reintroduces a dependency on campus Internet and a free-tier quota at exactly the moment of the demonstration |
| **The honesty rule** | The report must be able to state: *"All results in this chapter were produced by the locally-served model on [hardware]. No hosted inference service was used for any reported result."* If that sentence cannot be written truthfully, the contingency was used and must be disclosed |
| **By when** | **End of week 2** |

### D-01 — Cisco platform: the recommendation, and why **(needs ratification — B2)**

| | |
|---|---|
| **Decision** | Which platform hosts the Cisco layer that the AI plane must manage |
| **PRIMARY RECOMMENDATION** | **Cisco Modeling Labs (CML)**, smallest viable topology: one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall. Genuine IOS-XE/IOS images with **real SSH, real SNMPv3 and real RESTCONF**; the topology is the group's own and persists; and it is a **licensed Cisco product**, which is the option a school is most likely to permit |
| **FALLBACK** | **The hybrid: Packet Tracer for design plus 2–4 genuinely reachable devices** (a small physical set, or DevNet Sandbox instances used as a supplement). Keeps the whole existing Packet Tracer topology as the design artefact **and** guarantees the AI layer has devices it can truly manage |
| **REJECTED, with reasons** | **GNS3** and **EVE-NG**: free software and real interfaces, but the **IOS images raise a licensing/provenance question with no defensible answer for a graded deliverable**. **DevNet Sandbox alone**: free and real, but reserved instances are **time-boxed and reset**, so the built network is not an artefact you can submit, re-run at marking time or screenshot reproducibly. **Physical gear alone**: the strongest evidence, but it cannot host an ASA without buying one and classic IOS has **no RESTCONF**; excellent as a *supplement*. **Packet Tracer as the AI-managed layer: rejected outright** — it exposes **no reachable SSH, SNMP, NETCONF or RESTCONF and no API**, so nothing can manage it |
| **Full criteria analysis** | Section C.4 (nine criteria across six candidates) |
| **Where CML runs** | **On the CML host — named in B1, and not the same machine as the 19-VM estate host.** CML costs *"~ 16–32 GB RAM for a small topology on top of the hypervisor"* (§C.4), so B1's specification capture covers **two** hosts: the model host and the CML host, which may coincide only if the machine's RAM covers the model **and** CML's nodes **and** its own OS with margin. D-03 option (a) — *never co-locate the model with the 19-VM estate host* — is checked against both |
| **The Cisco device count (D-16)** | **Five devices as the CML target** — one HQ core pair, one WAN edge router, one branch router, one perimeter firewall — **up to eight only if every one is evidenced `REACHABLE` in §C.2b**; **2–4** under the hybrid fallback. This replaces the three figures that previously disagreed (§C.1's 5–8, §D.2's 5–6, §C.4's implied 5) |
| **The eBGP peer this decision does not place** | The AS-65000 **simulated provider** required by §B.3 C2 and §D.1.4 is a Packet Tracer object in the baseline (`§13.2 PT-09`) and is therefore **NR-01a — not reachable**. **Consequence, stated out loud: the AI layer can never verify the BGP session from the peer side**; only the local edge router's `show ip bgp summary` / `show ip route bgp` and its syslog/NetFlow are readable. A peer-side view requires a **second CML IOS-XE node as AS-65000**, which is a D-01/D-12/D-16 decision **taken in week 1** — *the decision*, not the build: it fixes the device count and the licence ask, and the node itself is built only after Stage C is evidenced. **This is §C.6's OPTIONAL Stage D, and it is explicitly not required within the 12 weeks:** its entry gate is *"Stage C is exited"*, so it falls in the **weeks 10–12** that remain after G4, and its cost (one more licence-entitled node plus CML RAM, §C.4) is why it is a bonus rather than a target |
| **If the school forbids everything but a simulator** | The scope reduction in **Section C.5** applies: the AI plane covers the **Linux and Windows estate only**, `MCP-S-CISCO` is dropped, the Cisco layer becomes design-and-verify, and the reduction is written down with its assessment consequences |
| **Also required** | **D-12**: confirm CML licence tiers, image entitlement and the RAM cost of a small topology in week 1. **Confirm at the vendor's own product and documentation pages — never from memory, a forum post or a mirror** |
| **By when** | **End of week 1** (B2) — this blocks `MCP-S-CISCO` and therefore one domain of the AI layer |

