| **Options** | (a) the five-device CML core; (b) five to eight devices; (c) the hybrid's 2–4 reachable devices only |
| **Recommendation** | **(a) five devices as the CML target**, rising to **at most eight** only if **every one of them can be evidenced as `REACHABLE` in §C.2b**; **2–4** under the D-01 hybrid fallback. **The matrix is normative**, so the count follows the evidence rather than the ambition |
| **Consequence of inflating the count** | More Cisco devices than can be evidenced inflate §C.2b's `REACHABLE` row count without adding a single verified interface — which is precisely the defect class §C.2b exists to prevent |
| **Also settled** | The estate sentence in §D.2 is corrected to the baseline's own **19 VMs / 10 Ubuntu / 9 Windows**, so §L.1a item 3's "carried forward verbatim" claim and §C.2b's matrix now agree on one number. **This count is also the target of §C.6's Stage C**, and the order in which the devices are brought under AI management — one (Stage B), then five (Stage C) — is fixed there: **the number may rise from one to five as transcripts are filed, and may never be raised by design intent.** §C.6's optional Stage D would add one further genuinely reachable node (the AS-65000 provider) and that addition is reconciled against this decision, not assumed |
| **By when** | **End of week 1** (with **B2**/D-01) |

