## K. Risks and mitigations

| ID | Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R1 | **The Cisco layer is not actually reachable**, so MCP-S-CISCO has nothing to manage and a third of the AI layer is lost | **High** | **High** | Gate **G1** at week 3: one device with working SSH **and** SNMPv3, with `show version` captured. **Gate G3 at week 8 is where §C.6's Stage B closes** — one Cisco device managed end to end by the AI layer. Fall through the D-01 options immediately rather than in week 6. If no option works, take the **§C.5** reduction (two families) and state it as a result rather than sliding into it silently. **§C.6's Stage D (peer-side eBGP, §C.2b NR-01a) is optional and is not a rescue path for this risk** | S1 |
| R2 | **CML licence or image entitlement cannot be obtained** | Medium | High | Check in week 1 (D-12); pre-position DevNet Sandbox as the fallback and a physical 1841/2960-class set as the second fallback | S1 |
| R3 | **Local model tool-calling is unreliable** — malformed or invented tool calls | **High** for small models | High | Make tool-calling **the** selection criterion; measure a malformed-call rate in the week-5/6 spike; prefer a larger model over a more ambitious use case; budget for a model swap at week 8 | S4 |
| R4 | **No GPU available at all** | Medium | High | CPU-only fallback design in Section E.1; script every run; drop UC-4; document the constraint as a stated limitation | S4 |
| R5 | **The estate does not fit the host** (RAM exhaustion with 19 VMs plus a model) | **High** | High | Never place the model inside the estate host; run only what a test needs; thin provisioning; the two-shift fallback for the VM estate | S2 |
| R6 | **Time**: 12 weeks is not enough for both layers at depth | **High** | High | The MUST/SHOULD/OPTIONAL split in Section D.2; gate G2 at week 6; the freeze rule (below) | all |
| R7 | **Documentation is a graded deliverable and is left to the end** | **High** | **High** | Documentation is a per-week activity from week 1; the caption and filename standards are enforced from the first screenshot; S4 integrates weekly, not at week 12; **Domain 6 (25%) explicitly requires a technical document** | S4 |
| R8 | **Prompt injection through device banners, log lines or descriptions** | Medium | Medium | Read-only surface (nothing to execute); tool output framed as untrusted data; a deliberate injection test in the evaluation set | S4 |
| R9 | **Credential leakage** into Git, a prompt or a transcript | Medium | **High** | Redaction at the MCP boundary; service identities with minimum rights; `0600` secrets files outside the repository; a pre-tag secret sweep; a transcript grep for credential patterns | S4 |
| R10 | **Version/URL/licence drift between the document and reality** | Medium | High | Every version captured from the running system; every URL archived with an access date; anything unconfirmed marked `[TO CONFIRM]` and closed before submission | all |
| R11 | **A promised use case is not demonstrated** | Medium | Medium | Commit to four, aim for six (D-06); the week-8 gate is where the list is formally cut, not week 11. **The cut has a stated floor: §B.3 C6's stage-based reduction means a project that closes at Stage B meets C6 and G4 with UC-3 and UC-6, so the reduction is reported as a result rather than as a missed promise** | S4 |
| R12 | **Packet Tracer is misrepresented as automatable** in a figure or a paragraph | Medium | **High** (integrity) | The crossed-out arrow in Figure 1; the verbatim sentence in Section C.4; a consistency sweep that searches the report for any automation claim attached to Packet Tracer | S1 |
| R13 | **A group member's machine or the single host fails mid-project** | Medium | Medium | Git on the management host **and** mirrored to a second medium; config archive; templates committed; no member is the sole holder of any artefact | all |
| R14 | **Scope creep into SDN, Kubernetes, or AI fine-tuning** | Medium | Medium | The explicit non-goals in Section B.4; the week-9 freeze: **after week 9 no new technology may be added — only testing and documenting** | S4 |
| R15 | **The AI layer produces a confidently wrong diagnosis and the group reports it as a success** | Medium | **High** (integrity) | Ground-truth fault injection; three runs per scenario with a reported pass rate; the hallucination check with a counted fabrication rate; a mandatory "failures and limitations" subsection | S4 |
| R16 | **The DevNet Sandbox resets or expires mid-evaluation** | Medium | Low | Use it only to supplement; never store a graded artefact there; never make it the environment of record | S1 |
| R17 | **The lab machine's specification is never recorded**, so the model is chosen on reputation | **High** | **High** | **B1 is a blocking action item with a dated gate: G2-H, end of week 6** (E.1.1, §F.1) — the date moved with SQ-2's deferral, and the requirement did not. No model commit before the written record exists; the ~20-probe tool-calling set is built in weeks 1–6 (§E.1.1a) so that it is ready to make the choice at the gate | S4 |
| R18 | **The lab machine is shared, bookable, or unavailable during the demonstration** | **High** | Medium | The booking rules depend on which machine it is, and the machine's identity is **B1's record at G2-H (week 6)** — so they are established at that gate rather than in week 1. What is done in week 1 is to **establish the rule that every evaluation run must be scripted and queueable in an available window**, which needs no hardware answer; **keep the T0 local fallback ready and documented** so the AI layer cannot become impossible | S4 |
| R19 | **The model and the VM estate are co-located**, so the model starves the network (or the reverse) and the failure looks like a network fault | Medium | High | **D-03 option (a) is mandatory**: model on the lab machine, estate elsewhere. State the rule; do not drift from it under time pressure | S2 + S4 |
| R20 | **The lab forbids installing the serving stack or blocks outbound access** needed to fetch model weights | Medium | Medium | **Ask at the G2-H gate, as part of B1's record** — *"what the lab permits installing"* and *"whether outbound network access is permitted and to where"* are two of B1's own fields, so the question is answered by the record itself rather than by a separate week-1 enquiry. If weights cannot be fetched, obtain them once through an allowed channel and carry them in on local media; if installation is forbidden entirely, D-13 falls to the documented contingency **or** the AI layer reduces to the Section C.5-style scope reduction for the model as well | S4 |
| R21 | **The read-only policy is quietly relaxed under deadline pressure** to "make the demo more impressive" | Medium | **High** (integrity) | The policy is a **student decision**, not a default: changing it requires a documented decision-log entry and a re-run of tests PB-2 … PB-5 and PB-9. Removing the gate must also show up in `git log`, because the gate lives in a version-controlled policy file | S4 |

**Freeze points.** No new technology after the end of week 9. No change to the committed use-case list after gate G4. No change to the evidence standard at any point — it is cheapest to follow from the first screenshot and most expensive to retrofit.

---

## L. What changes versus the previous design

The earlier frozen baseline (`proposal\00-design-baseline.md` Rev 1.1) was a careful design for a **different emphasis**: a broad, deeply documented enterprise network with a small, deliberately read-only AI proof of concept bolted on. The clarified intent makes the network the **foundation** and the AI management capability the **point**. The changes below follow from that, not from a dislike of the earlier work.

### L.1 Still usable — reuse it, and say that you are reusing it

| Carried over | Why it still serves the new direction |
|---|---|
| **The IP addressing plan** — `10.10.0.0/16` for the estate, `10.20.0.0/16` for management, `10.70.0.0/16` for the isolated lab, `172.16.0.0/16` unallocated, RFC 5737 ranges for simulated public space | It is already consistent, defensible and documented. Continuity is worth more than a redesign |
| **The VLAN plan and subnets** — VLAN 10/20/30/40/50/80/90 with their names and gateways | The zone model maps cleanly onto the management plane's needs; VLAN 20 is already the management zone the AI host belongs in |
| **Hostnames and naming conventions** — `VP-{SITE}-{ROLE}-{NN}` for devices, `vpnz-{role}{NN}` for VMs, the 15-character rule, the domain `vertexpeak.fyp.lab`, the banned-strings list | Continuity of naming is what keeps a four-person document consistent. Reuse verbatim |
| **The zone and trust-boundary model** — `Z-MGMT`, `Z-SERVER`, `Z-DMZ`, `Z-USER`, `Z-STUDENT`, `Z-EGRESS`, `Z-WAN`, `Z-REMOTE`, and the TB-01…TB-16 rules | The AI host is simply one more member of `Z-MGMT` with an explicit read-only rule, which the model already anticipated as TB-16 |
| **The service design** — AD DS/DNS/DHCP/NTP/PKI, FSRM, iSCSI, RRAS/OpenVPN, Nginx + Keepalived, nftables, monitoring with Prometheus/Grafana/Loki, the Ansible control node | Every one of these is *also* the substrate the AI layer reads. Nothing here is wasted |
| **The service identities** — `svc_aiagent` in `GG-AI-ReadOnly` only, `svc_monitoring`, `svc_ansible` | Already least-privilege and already separated; the MCP servers adopt them directly |
| **The repository layout, evidence naming and caption template** | Already correct; the MCP servers and the evaluation just need new folders |
| **The WSOS analysis and the citation discipline** | The verified extraction is the citation of record and remains authoritative |
| **The RACI role split** | Reused with the AI/Cisco emphasis shifted per D-05 |

### L.1a The highest-value artefacts to carry forward — nothing is wasted

So that the group can see explicitly what the 286k-word corpus has already bought, these are the artefacts that remain **valid and load-bearing** under the new direction. **Each one should be reused essentially as written**, and the group should say in the proposal that it is reusing them.

| # | Carried-forward artefact | Where it lives now | Why it survives unchanged |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **The IP / VLAN addressing plan** | baseline §4–§5; appendix A–B | Complete, internally consistent, and already justified against RFC 1918/5737 |
| 2 | **The naming conventions and the interface contract** | baseline §8 and §16 | It is the anti-inconsistency mechanism for a four-person document; re-deriving it would be pure loss |
| 3 | **The device and VM inventory** | baseline §6; appendix C | 36 rows, with roles, resources and NIC attachments already assigned. **Carried forward in full — 9 Windows Server guests, 10 Ubuntu guests and 1 hypervisor host, every one of them either a `REACHABLE`/`CONDITIONALLY REACHABLE` row or a named `NOT REACHABLE` row of the §C.2b matrix** (see §C.2b's estate-coverage table; this is the number §C.1, §D.2 and D-16 use) |
| 4 | **The service port and protocol matrix** | baseline §10 (P-01…P-35); appendix D | Needs one addition (a reachability column), but the service matrix itself is done |
| 5 | **The trust-boundary definitions TB-01…TB-16** | baseline §3.5 | Directly reusable, and TB-16 already describes the AI host's read-only rule |
| 6 | **The test-case ID scheme** | baseline §18 (`TC-{LINE}-{NN}`) | Extend with `TC-REACH-…` for the reachability matrix and keep the rest |
| 7 | **The evidence / screenshot standard** | baseline §14.2 rule 5; `08` §14 | Already correct; Section H.2 restates it |
| 8 | **The WSOS 2026 alignment, with its honest gap disclosure** | `01` §4.1–§4.3; `research\wsos-2026-2447.md` | The standard still contains no AI content; the disclosure stays valid and is the proposal's strongest integrity asset. Only the **conclusion** changes (see L.4) |
| 9 | **The AI chapter's reusable method and safety material** | `07` §11.8, §11.9 | Reusable as method, unchanged: the guardrail design; the read-only service accounts; the audit logging; the prompt-injection defence; the evaluation method (scripted scenarios, a measured manual baseline, a false-positive probe, a hallucination probe and an injection probe); the blast-radius analysis; and the human-in-the-loop boundary. Only the instances are replaced — the OpenClaw runtime and its four bespoke tools |
| 10 | **The verification and acceptance machinery** | `08` §13.1–§13.11 | Described in the gap analysis as the corpus's strongest single asset. Keep the method; add the reachability test family and re-base the AI acceptance gate |
| 11 | **The documentation and evidence management chapters** | `08` §14.1–§14.9 | Directly satisfies the brief's "findings, manuals, steps, screen captures, verifications, references" |
| 12 | **The "simulated versus genuinely configured" honesty paragraph** | baseline §11.7 | Still exactly right, and now it needs one edit: the Cisco layer moves from the "simulated" clause to the "genuinely configured" clause **if and only if** C.2b's RW-07/RW-08 rows are evidenced |

### L.2 Now off-target — change it deliberately

| Previously | Now | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **The entire Cisco layer inside Cisco Packet Tracer** | **Cisco devices with real SSH, SNMPv3 and RESTCONF**, in CML (or a licensed equivalent, a hosted sandbox, or physical gear) — **brought into the AI-managed set incrementally, one device at a time, per the staged ramp at §C.6** | Packet Tracer exposes no reachable management interface. The previous design's own limitation table admitted this (PT-06) and then designed an AI layer on top of it. That is the single most important correction in this document |
| **Network-device automation as a declared "two-track" workaround** — playbooks written but never executed against a device | **Real playbooks against real devices** over SSH, with captured idempotent runs | The workaround existed only because the devices were unreachable. With reachability, the workaround disappears |
| **AI as an optional, read-only "advisory proof of concept"** with five fallbacks, one of which was "demonstrate the deterministic half only" | **AI as the project's differentiating deliverable**, with MCP as a first-class, five-server tool plane and a committed use-case list | The clarified intent makes the AI management capability the point of the project, not an addendum |
| **OpenClaw as the agent runtime**, flagged as the single highest-uncertainty item with no version, URL or licence verified | **A local model plus MCP**, with named candidate model families and a serving stack, and **no dependency on an unverified third-party runtime** | The intent is "本地嘅 ai model，加 mcp". MCP is a published specification; a local model is under the group's control. This removes the project's largest single unknown |
| **No local model at all** ("no GPU; the agent uses either a small local model or an API — see the fallback") | **A local model as a requirement**, sized against measured hardware, with tool calling as the selection criterion | "本地" is explicit in the intent; an API dependency also means the demo breaks without the campus Internet |
| **CUCM Express voice, WordPress, and other breadth items** | **Cut to OPTIONAL or dropped** | Breadth was affordable when the AI layer was small. With MCP and an evaluation set to build, breadth competes directly with the differentiator |
| **A single 12-week plan that builds everything in parallel** | **A gated plan with services first and AI second**, freeze points, and a formal cut at G3 | The clarified sequence is explicit: 先建立網絡服務，然後再睇 AI 功能點樣寫 |

### L.3 New — does not exist in the previous design at all

1. **The reachable Cisco platform decision** (Sections C.4, C.5 and J-D-01) — a full nine-criterion comparison across six candidates, with a stated primary, a stated fallback, a rejection rationale for each alternative, and an honest scope reduction for the simulator-only case.
2. **MCP as the integration contract** — five servers, typed tool signatures, a read-only/mutating classification for every tool, and an explicit "must never" list per server.
3. **The local model layer** — a five-tier hardware-keyed sizing plan (T0 CPU-only through T4 multi-GPU) giving model class, quantisation, tool-calling reliability, expected tokens/second and demo sufficiency per tier; a **scheduled action item to capture the lab specification at the G2-H gate (week 6), not now**; and a **fallback path that works with no GPU at all**.
4. **The offline-versus-hybrid trade-off** (D-13), stated with its credibility, data-handling and demonstration-risk consequences rather than left implicit.
5. **The agent's permission model as a specification, not a caveat** — the read-only default, the six-field change proposal, the single-use approval token, the eleven-item must-never list, the tamper-evident audit log, and an explicit blast-radius statement.
6. **The permission-boundary test suite (PB-1 … PB-9)** — including the decisive **refusal tests**: a change attempted without approval must fail closed *and* be logged, and a system that cannot be shown to refuse has not been evaluated.
7. **The evaluation design** — a fixed scenario set, five injected faults with known ground truth, a hallucination check with a counted fabrication rate that **gates the write demonstration**, a false-positive check on the drift tool, and a human-timed baseline.
8. **A decided tool allow-list for Cisco** — the enumerated `show` commands that constitute the entire read surface.
9. **The drift baseline as a machine-readable artefact** (`MCP-S-DOC`) rather than a chapter in a document, which is what makes a compliance check something the agent can actually perform.
10. **The standards-alignment argument for the permission policy (I.8)** — read-only-by-default with human-approved change presented as professional practice that the WSOS's own Domain 1 and Domain 6 lines describe, not as a reduction of ambition.
11. **The project's honest boundary** — an explicit statement of what the AI observes, correlates, explains, proposes, and the single narrow action class it may execute with approval.
12. **The staged AI-coverage ramp (§C.6)** — the increment the student's SQ-1 answer requires. Four stages (A: the Linux and Windows estate; B: the first genuinely reachable Cisco device; C: the five-device D-16 target set; D: optional, peer-side eBGP verification), each with **entry preconditions, a stated demonstration, required evidence, an exit gate and the weeks it can fall in**, plus the mapping from the committed use cases (UC-1 … UC-6) to the earliest stage that can carry each one, and a **stated minimum acceptable stage (Stage B)** with its justification. Nothing in the previous design orders coverage as a gated ramp; the previous design ordered it as a Gantt bar.

### L.4 Statements in the existing design that this outline WITHDRAWS

These must be listed in the proposal's change record as **superseded**, with the reason, so that no later writer re-imports them. They are not errors of care — they were correct statements about a project whose emphasis was different — but they are now **false** about this one.

| # | Existing statement (verbatim or close paraphrase) | Location | **Status** | Replacement |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| W1 | `Model hosting / fine-tuning / GPU inference | NOT COVERED` | baseline **§11.5** (L5 table; §11.6 is the WSOS alignment table) | **WITHDRAWN.** Local model serving is now **core deliverable #3** | A local model served on identified hardware is a **MUST**; fine-tuning and trained analytics remain out of scope (Section B.4) |
| W2 | `GPU | Not required | Not required | No local GPU inference is planned` | baseline §15.2 (host specification) | **WITHDRAWN.** A GPU is now the **recommended target** | Target tier **T2 (12–16 GB VRAM)** on a school/lab machine, with a documented CPU-only fallback path (E.1.2, E.1.3) |
| W3 | *"Model hosting, fine-tuning, GPU inference and predictive analytics with trained models are also out of scope and appear only as future work"* | `08-project-management.md` §13.7 | **WITHDRAWN** as to model hosting and GPU inference | Model hosting and GPU inference are in scope; fine-tuning and trained predictive analytics remain future work |
| W4 | *"the AI line is the weakest line"* | `08` §12.5.2 (and the surrounding risk framing) | **WITHDRAWN — DEFINITIVELY, AND AS A RATIFIED POSITION RATHER THAN A RECOMMENDATION.** The student's answer to SQ-4 is 「你可以用呢個方式理解」 to the compound question that asked whether the AI/MCP layer is now the assessed differentiator *and* whether the old framing should be formally withdrawn. **Both halves are therefore decided:** the withdrawal is a **ratified statement of this project**, and the replacement framing that follows is **settled rather than proposed**. **No later writer may re-import this framing, restate it as a caveat, or hedge it as "arguably" — it is superseded, and the change record must say so.** The framing is incompatible with the project's purpose: a project whose point is AI network management cannot describe its AI line as its weakest line | **The AI / MCP line is THE ASSESSED DIFFERENTIATOR**, and the network is **the foundation it depends on** (SQ-4; §I.4; §L.2). This is a claim about what is being assessed, and it is argued honestly: the AI content is **outside** WSOS 2026 and is **never claimed as standard compliance** (§I.4, §I.6). The dominant AI risks change from "the runtime may not exist" to **"the model may not fit the hardware"** (now a **deferred decision with a dated gate**, G2-H — §A, §E.1.1, §J-D-04) and **"no device may be reachable"** (§C.2b NR-01/NR-01a; §C.6's Stage B/C gates) (Section K, R1/R3/R17) |
| W5 | The **"MCP tool bridge"** entry sitting in the **cut / optional band** | `08` §12.1, §12.9; baseline §13.4 R-02 | **WITHDRAWN.** MCP is not cuttable; it is the integration contract | MCP moves into the **MUST** band, and MCP tool surface completeness becomes an acceptance criterion (Section B.3, C5) |
| W6 | `P-34 | 18789/tcp | OpenClaw agent control/API endpoint` with the port marked **[ASSUMPTION]** | baseline §10 | **SUPERSEDED.** The design no longer depends on that runtime | Replace with the **MCP server endpoints and transport** actually used, with the real port read from the running service |
| W7 | The **five fallbacks F1–F5** and the **OpenClaw feasibility spike** | baseline §12.5; `08` | **WITHDRAWN.** They manage a risk this outline removes | Replaced by the model-tier fallback path (E.1.3) and the Section C.5 scope reduction |
| W8 | **The AI chapter's device-facing tool claims** — `get_interface_stats` as an *"SNMPv3 authPriv poll of the Packet Tracer device estate"*, the §11.6.4 data-flow diagram, and the §11.7.2 `snmpwalk …` example | `07-ai.md` §11.6.3, §11.6.4, §11.7.2 | **WITHDRAWN — factually wrong.** They contradict `06-automation.md` §10.7 and `05-cisco.md` §9.2.4 | Redraw every diagram so that **no arrow points at a Packet Tracer device**; re-express the tool against a device that appears as `REACHABLE` in Section C.2b, or delete it (Section C.2a) |
| W9 | **The compute budget for the AI host** — `vpnz-ai01` at **4 vCPU / 8 GB** | baseline §15.1, §6 | **SUPERSEDED.** That sizing cannot host a useful local model | Re-plan the compute budget **once the lab specification is captured (B1)**, and **do not co-locate the model with the 19-VM estate host** (J-D-03) |
| W10 | The **"AI is optional / innovation beyond the standard, therefore may be reduced"** conclusion | `01` §4.4; `07` §11.1.1 | **WITHDRAWN as a conclusion; the underlying fact is KEPT** | The fact stands: WSOS 2026 contains no AI content (Section I.4). The conclusion flips: *outside the standard* therefore **our contribution**, not *therefore optional* |
| W11 | The **"two-track" Cisco automation workaround** — configurations maintained as files, playbooks written but never executed against a device (Track A / Track B) | baseline §11.4 | **WITHDRAWN**, unless Section C.5's simulator-only reduction applies | With a reachable Cisco layer, playbooks run against real devices with captured idempotent runs. Under C.5 the two-track honesty discipline is retained — and it is retained *for the AI layer too*, which is exactly what W8 violated |

### L.5 Sequencing — the strict order is already in the existing plan (do not re-plan it)

The clarified intent's fourth point — *services first, AI second* — **is already present in the existing schedule**, and the group should say so rather than present the sequencing as a new plan:

- In `08-project-management.md`'s 12-week table and Gantt charts, the **AI line's bar does not start until week 7**, and the AI row is **absent from weeks 1–6** apart from scope definition, threat modelling and the (now withdrawn) feasibility spike.
- The baseline's own milestone plan puts the AI decision at week 6 and the AI demonstration at week 10 (`00-design-baseline.md` §17).

**What this outline changes is not the order but its status.** The order becomes an **explicit hard dependency** with named gates rather than an incidental property of a Gantt bar:

> **AI work is gated on named services being up, verified and logging.** Concretely: no MCP server may be written before the host or device it manages is (a) reachable, (b) authenticated, and (c) producing the telemetry that server's tools read. No agent may be run against the estate before at least one MCP server per committed use-case domain exists. No mutating tool may be registered before the refusal test (PB-2) passes.

That turns a schedule coincidence into a verifiable gate (G2 at week 6, G3 at week 8), which is what makes it defensible in the report. **§C.6's staged AI-coverage ramp is the same ordering expressed as coverage rather than as a calendar: Stage A cannot be claimed before the server estate is up and logging, Stage B cannot be claimed before one Cisco device is genuinely reachable, and Stage C cannot be claimed before five devices each have a filed transcript.** The gate at **G2-H (week 6)** belongs to the *model* half of the AI line, not to this ordering — it exists because the hardware answer is deferred (SQ-2), and §E.1.1a is what keeps the deferral from stalling the work.

---

## M. Immediate next steps (next 1–2 weeks)

Ordered. **Only ONE item blocks work starting now — item 2, the Cisco platform ratification (B2).** The hardware item (item 1) is now a **dated gate (G2-H, end of week 6) rather than a week-1 blocker**, because SQ-2's answer defers it until the student returns to Hong Kong; **§E.1.1a lists the substantial work that proceeds without it**, and **item 2a below is that work, not a holding pattern.**

1. **Record the lab machine's specification — gate G2-H, end of week 6 (B1).** One page, in the decision log: **GPU model, exact VRAM in GB, system RAM, free disk, CPU and cores, OS and version, whether the machine is shared/bookable/dedicated for the 12 weeks, what the lab permits you to install, and whether outbound network access is permitted and to where** — **for both hosts named at §C.4: the model host and the CML host.** Owner: S4. **The date moved; the requirement did not.** The model choice is blocked on this record, and the record is owed at a named gate, so the deferral cannot become an indefinite wait. **Understand the reason for the move: the hardware answer does not exist yet — it will be arranged when the student returns to Hong Kong — so asking for it in week 1 was asking for something that could not be supplied.**
2. **Ratify the Cisco platform — BLOCKING NOW (B2).** Take the primary recommendation (**CML**, smallest viable topology) and the fallback (**hybrid: Packet Tracer for design + 2–4 genuinely reachable devices**) to the supervisor and get a decision recorded, **including a decision on the simulator-only case** and its Section C.5 scope reduction. Owner: S1, end of week 1. **SQ-1 has already ratified the direction** — the Cisco layer must become genuinely AI-manageable, introduced incrementally — **so what is outstanding here is the platform record, not the principle.**
2a. **Start the hardware-agnostic work in week 1 and do not idle on the deferred hardware answer (§E.1.1a).** The five MCP servers and their tests, the agent scaffolding with the approval gate and audit log, the evaluation harness and the ~20-probe tool-calling set, and the evidence scaffolding are all **explicitly independent of the GPU specification**. **This is what makes the SQ-2 deferral costless: the probe set that will choose the model at G2-H is built in weeks 1–6, while the machine is still undecided.** Owner: S4.
3. **Check the CML licence, image entitlement and topology RAM cost the same day (D-12).** Confirm at the vendor's own product and documentation pages — **never from memory, a forum post or a mirror** — and record the tier, the images included and the terms relied on. If this fails, move to the fallback immediately rather than at week 6.
4. **Confirm what the school's rules permit** — a licensed platform, personal physical equipment, and a hosted sandbox are three different permissions and may be granted differently. Ask all three questions now; discovering a prohibition in week 6 costs the AI demonstration.
5. **Stand up the repository and the evidence scaffolding in parallel with 1–4**: the folder tree, the Git repository, the figure-caption template and the filename convention. Freeze the evidence standard now, while there are zero screenshots to re-caption.
6. **Answer D-05 (Windows vs Cisco depth), D-06 (committed use cases), D-13 (offline vs hybrid), D-14 (hypervisor platform), D-15 (remote access: OpenVPN or IKEv2/RRAS) and D-16 (the Cisco device count)**, and print the RACI from Section G so that all four members know what they own before week 2 begins. **D-14 is required at G0**, so it cannot be deferred past week 1.
7. **Prove one Cisco device is reachable as the week-3 gate, on a single device.** Do not design the topology first. A single device with a working SSH login and one successful `snmpwalk -v3`, with `show version` captured, de-risks more of this project than any amount of design work.
8. **Write the permission policy into the proposal verbatim**, with the six-field change proposal, the single-use token, the must-never list and the audit-log requirement — and **build test PB-2 (the refusal test) at the same time as the first mutating tool**, not after it. A gate added later is a gate that was not there when it mattered.
9. **Book the local-model spike (weeks 5–6) on the calendar now, with its output tied to the G2-H gate**, with an explicit output: *a decision, a measured malformed-tool-call rate, and a named model.* **The spike's input is the G2-H hardware record** (§E.1.1), so if the record has not arrived by week 6, run the probe set against the T0/T1 candidates and let the measurement choose — an open-ended spike becomes an open-ended project, and **a spike that waits for a machine that has not been booked is the same failure wearing a different hat**.
10. **Start the device-reachability matrix (Section C.2b) on day 1, and file a transcript for every row you can.** This is the cheapest possible de-risking: run `Test-WSMan` against one Windows guest, `ssh` one Linux guest, `snmpwalk -v3` one Cisco device, and check one Prometheus target — then write down what answered. **Every row you cannot evidence is a row the AI layer cannot use**, and knowing that in week 1 rather than week 9 is the whole point.
11. **Draft the MUST-only service list from Section D.2 as a one-page checklist** and put it on the wall. It is the contract for what "the network is built" means, and it is what gate G2 will be judged against.
12. **Find and delete every Packet Tracer automation claim in the existing corpus.** Do this before any new writing: `07-ai.md` §11.6.3, §11.6.4 and §11.7.2 contradict `06-automation.md` §10.7, and a reader who finds one contradiction will doubt the whole document. The change record in Section L.4 is the checklist.
13. **Write the proposal — the immediate submission tier — from the decision material, not by pruning this outline.** SQ-5's answer is 「暫時只做 proposal」: **the proposal is the only thing being submitted now**, and it is where the 15–25-page band at §H.3 applies. **§F.2 now carries it as its own row (weeks 2–3), placed after G0 and clear of the G1/G2/G2-H gates**, so the schedule delivers the immediate submission rather than leaving it to this list alone. Section A's decisions and §C.6's ramp are the content a supervisor has to accept; everything else in this outline is reference material. **The full document at the 90,000–110,000-word scale is a later tier and is not written now.** *(An initial report is expected to follow the proposal in the programme's normal sequence; it is recorded at §H.3 as a likely future deliverable, not current scope, and its structure is `[TO CONFIRM]` against the programme handbook.)*

